{"function_name": "m3ApiRawFunction", "code_before": "m3ApiRawFunction(m3_wasi_generic_fd_read)\n{\n    m3ApiReturnType  (uint32_t)\n    m3ApiGetArg      (__wasi_fd_t          , fd)\n    m3ApiGetArgMem   (wasi_iovec_t *       , wasi_iovs)\n    m3ApiGetArg      (__wasi_size_t        , iovs_len)\n    m3ApiGetArgMem   (__wasi_size_t *      , nread)\n\n    m3ApiCheckMem(wasi_iovs,    iovs_len * sizeof(wasi_iovec_t));\n    m3ApiCheckMem(nread,        sizeof(__wasi_size_t));\n\n#if defined(HAS_IOVEC)\n    struct iovec iovs[iovs_len];\n    copy_iov_to_host(_mem, iovs, wasi_iovs, iovs_len);\n\n    ssize_t ret = readv(fd, iovs, iovs_len);\n    if (ret < 0) { m3ApiReturn(errno_to_wasi(errno)); }\n    m3ApiWriteMem32(nread, ret);\n    m3ApiReturn(__WASI_ERRNO_SUCCESS);\n#else\n    ssize_t res = 0;\n    for (__wasi_size_t i = 0; i < iovs_len; i++) {\n        void* addr = m3ApiOffsetToPtr(m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iovs[i].buf));\n        size_t len = m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iovs[i].buf_len);\n        if (len == 0) continue;\n\n        int ret = read (fd, addr, len);\n        if (ret < 0) m3ApiReturn(errno_to_wasi(errno));\n        res += ret;\n        if ((size_t)ret < len) break;\n    }\n    m3ApiWriteMem32(nread, res);\n    m3ApiReturn(__WASI_ERRNO_SUCCESS);\n#endif\n}\n", "code_after": "m3ApiRawFunction(m3_wasi_generic_fd_read)\n{\n    m3ApiReturnType  (uint32_t)\n    m3ApiGetArg      (__wasi_fd_t          , fd)\n    m3ApiGetArgMem   (wasi_iovec_t *       , wasi_iovs)\n    m3ApiGetArg      (__wasi_size_t        , iovs_len)\n    m3ApiGetArgMem   (__wasi_size_t *      , nread)\n\n    m3ApiCheckMem(wasi_iovs,    iovs_len * sizeof(wasi_iovec_t));\n    m3ApiCheckMem(nread,        sizeof(__wasi_size_t));\n\n#if defined(HAS_IOVEC)\n    struct iovec iovs[iovs_len];\n    const void* mem_check = copy_iov_to_host(runtime, _mem, iovs, wasi_iovs, iovs_len);\n    if (mem_check != m3Err_none) {\n        return mem_check;\n    }\n\n    ssize_t ret = readv(fd, iovs, iovs_len);\n    if (ret < 0) { m3ApiReturn(errno_to_wasi(errno)); }\n    m3ApiWriteMem32(nread, ret);\n    m3ApiReturn(__WASI_ERRNO_SUCCESS);\n#else\n    ssize_t res = 0;\n    for (__wasi_size_t i = 0; i < iovs_len; i++) {\n        void* addr = m3ApiOffsetToPtr(m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iovs[i].buf));\n        size_t len = m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iovs[i].buf_len);\n        if (len == 0) continue;\n        m3ApiCheckMem(addr,     len);\n        int ret = read (fd, addr, len);\n        if (ret < 0) m3ApiReturn(errno_to_wasi(errno));\n        res += ret;\n        if ((size_t)ret < len) break;\n    }\n    m3ApiWriteMem32(nread, res);\n    m3ApiReturn(__WASI_ERRNO_SUCCESS);\n#endif\n}\n", "commit_message": "fix WASI API read/write pread/pwrite buffer overflow (#324)\n\nCo-authored-by: \u8d75\u6d77 <zhaohai@cyberpeace.cn>", "commit": "https://github.com/wasm3/wasm3/commit/8441c82c4a98f554b12971450220b580c3c2b6df", "description": "WASM3 v0.5.0 was discovered to contain a heap overflow via the component /wabt/bin/poc.wasm.", "file_change_id": 27768485465453, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-77fq-4xf5-hph4", "programming_language": "C", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "copy_iov_to_host", "code_before": "void copy_iov_to_host(void* _mem, struct iovec* host_iov, wasi_iovec_t* wasi_iov, int32_t iovs_len)\n{\n    // Convert wasi memory offsets to host addresses\n    for (int i = 0; i < iovs_len; i++) {\n        host_iov[i].iov_base = m3ApiOffsetToPtr(m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iov[i].buf));\n        host_iov[i].iov_len  = m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iov[i].buf_len);\n    }\n}\n", "code_after": "const void* copy_iov_to_host(IM3Runtime runtime, void* _mem, struct iovec* host_iov, wasi_iovec_t* wasi_iov, int32_t iovs_len)\n{\n    // Convert wasi memory offsets to host addresses\n    for (int i = 0; i < iovs_len; i++) {\n        host_iov[i].iov_base = m3ApiOffsetToPtr(m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iov[i].buf));\n        host_iov[i].iov_len  = m3ApiReadMem32(&wasi_iov[i].buf_len);\n        m3ApiCheckMem(host_iov[i].iov_base,     host_iov[i].iov_len);\n    }\n    m3ApiSuccess();\n}\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `m3ApiRawFunction(m3_wasi_generic_fd_read)` has been modified to address a heap overflow vulnerability. The changes in the function are primarily related to memory checks and error handling.\n\nIn the `code_after` snippet, an additional memory check `m3ApiCheckMem(addr, len);` has been added in the `#else` section of the code. This check is likely to prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that the memory area being read is within the allocated bounds.\n\nMoreover, the function `copy_iov_to_host` has been modified to return a value, and this returned value is checked in the `m3ApiRawFunction(m3_wasi_generic_fd_read)` function. This change is likely to handle any errors that might occur in the `copy_iov_to_host` function and prevent them from causing a buffer overflow in the `m3ApiRawFunction(m3_wasi_generic_fd_read)` function.\n\nGiven these changes, it can be inferred that the function `m3ApiRawFunction(m3_wasi_generic_fd_read)` was vulnerable and has been modified to address the reported vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "__init__", "code_before": "    def __init__(self, *, openapi: GeneratorData) -> None:\n        self.openapi: GeneratorData = openapi\n        self.env: Environment = Environment(loader=PackageLoader(__package__), trim_blocks=True, lstrip_blocks=True)\n\n        self.project_name: str = self.project_name_override or f\"{utils.kebab_case(openapi.title).lower()}-client\"\n        self.project_dir: Path = Path.cwd() / self.project_name\n\n        self.package_name: str = self.package_name_override or self.project_name.replace(\"-\", \"_\")\n        self.package_dir: Path = self.project_dir / self.package_name\n        self.package_description: str = f\"A client library for accessing {self.openapi.title}\"\n        self.version: str = openapi.version\n\n        self.env.filters.update(self.TEMPLATE_FILTERS)\n", "code_after": "    def __init__(self, *, openapi: GeneratorData) -> None:\n        self.openapi: GeneratorData = openapi\n        self.env: Environment = Environment(loader=PackageLoader(__package__), trim_blocks=True, lstrip_blocks=True)\n\n        self.project_name: str = self.project_name_override or f\"{utils.kebab_case(openapi.title).lower()}-client\"\n        self.project_dir: Path = Path.cwd() / self.project_name\n\n        self.package_name: str = self.package_name_override or self.project_name.replace(\"-\", \"_\")\n        self.package_dir: Path = self.project_dir / self.package_name\n        self.package_description: str = utils.remove_string_escapes(\n            f\"A client library for accessing {self.openapi.title}\"\n        )\n        self.version: str = openapi.version\n\n        self.env.filters.update(self.TEMPLATE_FILTERS)\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 148128802634992, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the \"__init__\" function is being modified in a commit that addresses a security vulnerability. The vulnerability is described as allowing the generation of arbitrary Python code from a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document, which could lead to arbitrary code execution when the malicious client is executed.\n\nThe change in the \"__init__\" function is the addition of a call to `utils.remove_string_escapes()` when setting `self.package_description`. This change appears to be part of the mitigation for the vulnerability, as it could prevent the execution of arbitrary code by removing string escapes from the package description.\n\nGiven this, it seems that the \"__init__\" function was vulnerable and has been changed to address the vulnerability. Therefore, the answer is 1.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "from_data", "code_before": "    def from_data(*, data: oai.Operation, path: str, method: str, tag: str) -> Union[Endpoint, ParseError]:\n        \"\"\" Construct an endpoint from the OpenAPI data \"\"\"\n\n        if data.operationId is None:\n            return ParseError(data=data, detail=\"Path operations with operationId are not yet supported\")\n\n        endpoint = Endpoint(\n            path=path,\n            method=method,\n            description=data.description,\n            name=data.operationId,\n            requires_security=bool(data.security),\n            tag=tag,\n        )\n\n        result = Endpoint._add_parameters(endpoint, data)\n        if isinstance(result, ParseError):\n            return result\n        result = Endpoint._add_responses(result, data.responses)\n        if isinstance(result, ParseError):\n            return result\n        result = Endpoint._add_body(result, data)\n\n        return result\n", "code_after": "    def from_data(*, data: oai.Operation, path: str, method: str, tag: str) -> Union[Endpoint, ParseError]:\n        \"\"\" Construct an endpoint from the OpenAPI data \"\"\"\n\n        if data.operationId is None:\n            return ParseError(data=data, detail=\"Path operations with operationId are not yet supported\")\n\n        endpoint = Endpoint(\n            path=path,\n            method=method,\n            description=utils.remove_string_escapes(data.description) if data.description else \"\",\n            name=data.operationId,\n            requires_security=bool(data.security),\n            tag=tag,\n        )\n\n        result = Endpoint._add_parameters(endpoint, data)\n        if isinstance(result, ParseError):\n            return result\n        result = Endpoint._add_responses(result, data.responses)\n        if isinstance(result, ParseError):\n            return result\n        result = Endpoint._add_body(result, data)\n\n        return result\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 213667226242901, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `from_data` is being modified to address a security vulnerability. The vulnerability is described as allowing arbitrary Python code execution when clients are generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document.\n\nThe change in the `from_data` function is related to the `description` attribute of the `Endpoint` object. In the updated code, the `description` attribute is passed through a `utils.remove_string_escapes` function if it exists. This change is likely to sanitize the `description` to prevent the execution of arbitrary code.\n\nGiven the nature of the vulnerability and the changes made to the function, it can be inferred that the `from_data` function was vulnerable and has been modified to address the vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "property_from_data", "code_before": "def property_from_data(\n    name: str, required: bool, data: Union[oai.Reference, oai.Schema]\n) -> Union[Property, PropertyError]:\n    \"\"\" Generate a Property from the OpenAPI dictionary representation of it \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(data, oai.Reference):\n        return RefProperty(name=name, required=required, reference=Reference.from_ref(data.ref), default=None)\n    if data.enum:\n        return EnumProperty(\n            name=name,\n            required=required,\n            values=EnumProperty.values_from_list(data.enum),\n            title=data.title or name,\n            default=data.default,\n        )\n    if data.anyOf:\n        sub_properties: List[Property] = []\n        for sub_prop_data in data.anyOf:\n            sub_prop = property_from_data(name=name, required=required, data=sub_prop_data)\n            if isinstance(sub_prop, PropertyError):\n                return PropertyError(detail=f\"Invalid property in union {name}\", data=sub_prop_data)\n            sub_properties.append(sub_prop)\n        return UnionProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default, inner_properties=sub_properties)\n    if not data.type:\n        return PropertyError(data=data, detail=\"Schemas must either have one of enum, anyOf, or type defined.\")\n    if data.type == \"string\":\n        return _string_based_property(name=name, required=required, data=data)\n    elif data.type == \"number\":\n        return FloatProperty(name=name, default=data.default, required=required)\n    elif data.type == \"integer\":\n        return IntProperty(name=name, default=data.default, required=required)\n    elif data.type == \"boolean\":\n        return BooleanProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default)\n    elif data.type == \"array\":\n        if data.items is None:\n            return PropertyError(data=data, detail=\"type array must have items defined\")\n        inner_prop = property_from_data(name=f\"{name}_item\", required=True, data=data.items)\n        if isinstance(inner_prop, PropertyError):\n            return PropertyError(data=inner_prop.data, detail=f\"invalid data in items of array {name}\")\n        return ListProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default, inner_property=inner_prop,)\n    elif data.type == \"object\":\n        return DictProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default)\n    return PropertyError(data=data, detail=f\"unknown type {data.type}\")\n", "code_after": "def property_from_data(\n    name: str, required: bool, data: Union[oai.Reference, oai.Schema]\n) -> Union[Property, PropertyError]:\n    try:\n        return _property_from_data(name=name, required=required, data=data)\n    except ValidationError:\n        return PropertyError(detail=\"Failed to validate default value\", data=data)\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 84078200208367, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "__post_init__", "code_before": "    def __post_init__(self, title: str) -> None:  # type: ignore\n        super().__post_init__()\n        reference = Reference.from_ref(title)\n        dedup_counter = 0\n        while reference.class_name in _existing_enums:\n            existing = _existing_enums[reference.class_name]\n            if self.values == existing.values:\n                break  # This is the same Enum, we're good\n            dedup_counter += 1\n            reference = Reference.from_ref(f\"{reference.class_name}{dedup_counter}\")\n\n        self.reference = reference\n        inverse_values = {v: k for k, v in self.values.items()}\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = f\"{self.reference.class_name}.{inverse_values[self.default]}\"\n        _existing_enums[self.reference.class_name] = self\n", "code_after": "    def __post_init__(self, title: str) -> None:  # type: ignore\n        reference = Reference.from_ref(title)\n        dedup_counter = 0\n        while reference.class_name in _existing_enums:\n            existing = _existing_enums[reference.class_name]\n            if self.values == existing.values:\n                break  # This is the same Enum, we're good\n            dedup_counter += 1\n            reference = Reference.from_ref(f\"{reference.class_name}{dedup_counter}\")\n\n        self.reference = reference\n        super().__post_init__()\n        _existing_enums[self.reference.class_name] = self\n"}, {"function_name": "values_from_list", "code_before": "    def values_from_list(values: List[str]) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\" Convert a list of values into dict of {name: value} \"\"\"\n        output: Dict[str, str] = {}\n\n        for i, value in enumerate(values):\n            if value[0].isalpha():\n                key = value.upper()\n            else:\n                key = f\"VALUE_{i}\"\n            if key in output:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate key {key} in Enum\")\n            output[key] = value\n\n        return output\n", "code_after": "    def values_from_list(values: List[str]) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\" Convert a list of values into dict of {name: value} \"\"\"\n        output: Dict[str, str] = {}\n\n        for i, value in enumerate(values):\n            if value[0].isalpha():\n                key = value.upper()\n            else:\n                key = f\"VALUE_{i}\"\n            if key in output:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate key {key} in Enum\")\n            sanitized_key = utils.fix_keywords(utils.sanitize(key))\n            output[sanitized_key] = utils.remove_string_escapes(value)\n\n        return output\n"}, {"function_name": "get_imports", "code_before": "    def get_imports(self, *, prefix: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get a set of import strings that should be included when this property is used somewhere\n\n        Args:\n            prefix: A prefix to put before any relative (local) module names.\n        \"\"\"\n        imports = super().get_imports(prefix=prefix)\n        imports.update({\"from datetime import datetime\", \"from typing import cast\"})\n        return imports\n", "code_after": "    def get_imports(self, *, prefix: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get a set of import strings that should be included when this property is used somewhere\n\n        Args:\n            prefix: A prefix to put before any relative (local) module names.\n        \"\"\"\n        imports = super().get_imports(prefix=prefix)\n        imports.update({\"import datetime\", \"from typing import cast\"})\n        return imports\n"}, {"function_name": "__post_init__", "code_before": "    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        super().__post_init__()\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = f'\"{self.default}\"'\n", "code_after": "    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        self.python_name = utils.snake_case(self.name)\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = self._validate_default(default=self.default)\n"}, {"function_name": "get_type_string", "code_before": "    def get_type_string(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\" Get a string representation of type that should be used when declaring this property \"\"\"\n        if self.required:\n            return self._type_string\n        return f\"Optional[{self._type_string}]\"\n", "code_after": "    def get_type_string(self, no_optional: bool = False) -> str:\n        \"\"\" Get a string representation of type that should be used when declaring this property \"\"\"\n        if self.required or no_optional:\n            return self._type_string\n        return f\"Optional[{self._type_string}]\"\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The function \"property_from_data\" is changed to add a try-except block to catch a ValidationError and return a PropertyError. This change is related to the vulnerability described in the advisory, which is about arbitrary code execution due to a lack of validation. The commit message also mentions adding validation for default property values where possible, which is exactly what the change in this function is doing.\n\nTherefore, it can be inferred that the function \"property_from_data\" was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "values_from_list", "code_before": "    def values_from_list(values: List[str]) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\" Convert a list of values into dict of {name: value} \"\"\"\n        output: Dict[str, str] = {}\n\n        for i, value in enumerate(values):\n            if value[0].isalpha():\n                key = value.upper()\n            else:\n                key = f\"VALUE_{i}\"\n            if key in output:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate key {key} in Enum\")\n            output[key] = value\n\n        return output\n", "code_after": "    def values_from_list(values: List[str]) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\" Convert a list of values into dict of {name: value} \"\"\"\n        output: Dict[str, str] = {}\n\n        for i, value in enumerate(values):\n            if value[0].isalpha():\n                key = value.upper()\n            else:\n                key = f\"VALUE_{i}\"\n            if key in output:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate key {key} in Enum\")\n            sanitized_key = utils.fix_keywords(utils.sanitize(key))\n            output[sanitized_key] = utils.remove_string_escapes(value)\n\n        return output\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 84078200208367, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "__post_init__", "code_before": "    def __post_init__(self, title: str) -> None:  # type: ignore\n        super().__post_init__()\n        reference = Reference.from_ref(title)\n        dedup_counter = 0\n        while reference.class_name in _existing_enums:\n            existing = _existing_enums[reference.class_name]\n            if self.values == existing.values:\n                break  # This is the same Enum, we're good\n            dedup_counter += 1\n            reference = Reference.from_ref(f\"{reference.class_name}{dedup_counter}\")\n\n        self.reference = reference\n        inverse_values = {v: k for k, v in self.values.items()}\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = f\"{self.reference.class_name}.{inverse_values[self.default]}\"\n        _existing_enums[self.reference.class_name] = self\n", "code_after": "    def __post_init__(self, title: str) -> None:  # type: ignore\n        reference = Reference.from_ref(title)\n        dedup_counter = 0\n        while reference.class_name in _existing_enums:\n            existing = _existing_enums[reference.class_name]\n            if self.values == existing.values:\n                break  # This is the same Enum, we're good\n            dedup_counter += 1\n            reference = Reference.from_ref(f\"{reference.class_name}{dedup_counter}\")\n\n        self.reference = reference\n        super().__post_init__()\n        _existing_enums[self.reference.class_name] = self\n"}, {"function_name": "property_from_data", "code_before": "def property_from_data(\n    name: str, required: bool, data: Union[oai.Reference, oai.Schema]\n) -> Union[Property, PropertyError]:\n    \"\"\" Generate a Property from the OpenAPI dictionary representation of it \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(data, oai.Reference):\n        return RefProperty(name=name, required=required, reference=Reference.from_ref(data.ref), default=None)\n    if data.enum:\n        return EnumProperty(\n            name=name,\n            required=required,\n            values=EnumProperty.values_from_list(data.enum),\n            title=data.title or name,\n            default=data.default,\n        )\n    if data.anyOf:\n        sub_properties: List[Property] = []\n        for sub_prop_data in data.anyOf:\n            sub_prop = property_from_data(name=name, required=required, data=sub_prop_data)\n            if isinstance(sub_prop, PropertyError):\n                return PropertyError(detail=f\"Invalid property in union {name}\", data=sub_prop_data)\n            sub_properties.append(sub_prop)\n        return UnionProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default, inner_properties=sub_properties)\n    if not data.type:\n        return PropertyError(data=data, detail=\"Schemas must either have one of enum, anyOf, or type defined.\")\n    if data.type == \"string\":\n        return _string_based_property(name=name, required=required, data=data)\n    elif data.type == \"number\":\n        return FloatProperty(name=name, default=data.default, required=required)\n    elif data.type == \"integer\":\n        return IntProperty(name=name, default=data.default, required=required)\n    elif data.type == \"boolean\":\n        return BooleanProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default)\n    elif data.type == \"array\":\n        if data.items is None:\n            return PropertyError(data=data, detail=\"type array must have items defined\")\n        inner_prop = property_from_data(name=f\"{name}_item\", required=True, data=data.items)\n        if isinstance(inner_prop, PropertyError):\n            return PropertyError(data=inner_prop.data, detail=f\"invalid data in items of array {name}\")\n        return ListProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default, inner_property=inner_prop,)\n    elif data.type == \"object\":\n        return DictProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default)\n    return PropertyError(data=data, detail=f\"unknown type {data.type}\")\n", "code_after": "def property_from_data(\n    name: str, required: bool, data: Union[oai.Reference, oai.Schema]\n) -> Union[Property, PropertyError]:\n    try:\n        return _property_from_data(name=name, required=required, data=data)\n    except ValidationError:\n        return PropertyError(detail=\"Failed to validate default value\", data=data)\n"}, {"function_name": "get_imports", "code_before": "    def get_imports(self, *, prefix: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get a set of import strings that should be included when this property is used somewhere\n\n        Args:\n            prefix: A prefix to put before any relative (local) module names.\n        \"\"\"\n        imports = super().get_imports(prefix=prefix)\n        imports.update({\"from datetime import datetime\", \"from typing import cast\"})\n        return imports\n", "code_after": "    def get_imports(self, *, prefix: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get a set of import strings that should be included when this property is used somewhere\n\n        Args:\n            prefix: A prefix to put before any relative (local) module names.\n        \"\"\"\n        imports = super().get_imports(prefix=prefix)\n        imports.update({\"import datetime\", \"from typing import cast\"})\n        return imports\n"}, {"function_name": "__post_init__", "code_before": "    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        super().__post_init__()\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = f'\"{self.default}\"'\n", "code_after": "    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        self.python_name = utils.snake_case(self.name)\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = self._validate_default(default=self.default)\n"}, {"function_name": "get_type_string", "code_before": "    def get_type_string(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\" Get a string representation of type that should be used when declaring this property \"\"\"\n        if self.required:\n            return self._type_string\n        return f\"Optional[{self._type_string}]\"\n", "code_after": "    def get_type_string(self, no_optional: bool = False) -> str:\n        \"\"\" Get a string representation of type that should be used when declaring this property \"\"\"\n        if self.required or no_optional:\n            return self._type_string\n        return f\"Optional[{self._type_string}]\"\n"}], "gpt_answer": "\n    The function \"values_from_list\" is changed to sanitize the keys and values of the dictionary it returns. This is done by using the \"utils.fix_keywords\" and \"utils.sanitize\" functions for the keys and \"utils.remove_string_escapes\" for the values.\n\nThe vulnerability description mentions that clients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code, leading to arbitrary code execution. The changes in the function seem to be directly addressing this issue by sanitizing the keys and values, which could potentially be part of the maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document.\n\nTherefore, it can be concluded that the function \"values_from_list\" was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "__post_init__", "code_before": "    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        super().__post_init__()\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = f'\"{self.default}\"'\n", "code_after": "    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        self.python_name = utils.snake_case(self.name)\n        if self.default is not None:\n            self.default = self._validate_default(default=self.default)\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 84078200208367, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "property_from_data", "code_before": "def property_from_data(\n    name: str, required: bool, data: Union[oai.Reference, oai.Schema]\n) -> Union[Property, PropertyError]:\n    \"\"\" Generate a Property from the OpenAPI dictionary representation of it \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(data, oai.Reference):\n        return RefProperty(name=name, required=required, reference=Reference.from_ref(data.ref), default=None)\n    if data.enum:\n        return EnumProperty(\n            name=name,\n            required=required,\n            values=EnumProperty.values_from_list(data.enum),\n            title=data.title or name,\n            default=data.default,\n        )\n    if data.anyOf:\n        sub_properties: List[Property] = []\n        for sub_prop_data in data.anyOf:\n            sub_prop = property_from_data(name=name, required=required, data=sub_prop_data)\n            if isinstance(sub_prop, PropertyError):\n                return PropertyError(detail=f\"Invalid property in union {name}\", data=sub_prop_data)\n            sub_properties.append(sub_prop)\n        return UnionProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default, inner_properties=sub_properties)\n    if not data.type:\n        return PropertyError(data=data, detail=\"Schemas must either have one of enum, anyOf, or type defined.\")\n    if data.type == \"string\":\n        return _string_based_property(name=name, required=required, data=data)\n    elif data.type == \"number\":\n        return FloatProperty(name=name, default=data.default, required=required)\n    elif data.type == \"integer\":\n        return IntProperty(name=name, default=data.default, required=required)\n    elif data.type == \"boolean\":\n        return BooleanProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default)\n    elif data.type == \"array\":\n        if data.items is None:\n            return PropertyError(data=data, detail=\"type array must have items defined\")\n        inner_prop = property_from_data(name=f\"{name}_item\", required=True, data=data.items)\n        if isinstance(inner_prop, PropertyError):\n            return PropertyError(data=inner_prop.data, detail=f\"invalid data in items of array {name}\")\n        return ListProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default, inner_property=inner_prop,)\n    elif data.type == \"object\":\n        return DictProperty(name=name, required=required, default=data.default)\n    return PropertyError(data=data, detail=f\"unknown type {data.type}\")\n", "code_after": "def property_from_data(\n    name: str, required: bool, data: Union[oai.Reference, oai.Schema]\n) -> Union[Property, PropertyError]:\n    try:\n        return _property_from_data(name=name, required=required, data=data)\n    except ValidationError:\n        return PropertyError(detail=\"Failed to validate default value\", data=data)\n"}, {"function_name": "values_from_list", "code_before": "    def values_from_list(values: List[str]) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\" Convert a list of values into dict of {name: value} \"\"\"\n        output: Dict[str, str] = {}\n\n        for i, value in enumerate(values):\n            if value[0].isalpha():\n                key = value.upper()\n            else:\n                key = f\"VALUE_{i}\"\n            if key in output:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate key {key} in Enum\")\n            output[key] = value\n\n        return output\n", "code_after": "    def values_from_list(values: List[str]) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\" Convert a list of values into dict of {name: value} \"\"\"\n        output: Dict[str, str] = {}\n\n        for i, value in enumerate(values):\n            if value[0].isalpha():\n                key = value.upper()\n            else:\n                key = f\"VALUE_{i}\"\n            if key in output:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate key {key} in Enum\")\n            sanitized_key = utils.fix_keywords(utils.sanitize(key))\n            output[sanitized_key] = utils.remove_string_escapes(value)\n\n        return output\n"}, {"function_name": "get_imports", "code_before": "    def get_imports(self, *, prefix: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get a set of import strings that should be included when this property is used somewhere\n\n        Args:\n            prefix: A prefix to put before any relative (local) module names.\n        \"\"\"\n        imports = super().get_imports(prefix=prefix)\n        imports.update({\"from datetime import datetime\", \"from typing import cast\"})\n        return imports\n", "code_after": "    def get_imports(self, *, prefix: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Get a set of import strings that should be included when this property is used somewhere\n\n        Args:\n            prefix: A prefix to put before any relative (local) module names.\n        \"\"\"\n        imports = super().get_imports(prefix=prefix)\n        imports.update({\"import datetime\", \"from typing import cast\"})\n        return imports\n"}, {"function_name": "get_type_string", "code_before": "    def get_type_string(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\" Get a string representation of type that should be used when declaring this property \"\"\"\n        if self.required:\n            return self._type_string\n        return f\"Optional[{self._type_string}]\"\n", "code_after": "    def get_type_string(self, no_optional: bool = False) -> str:\n        \"\"\" Get a string representation of type that should be used when declaring this property \"\"\"\n        if self.required or no_optional:\n            return self._type_string\n        return f\"Optional[{self._type_string}]\"\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the vulnerability in question pertains to arbitrary code execution due to unsanitized strings used as file/directory names. The commit message suggests that the fix involves sanitizing these strings and adding validation for default property values.\n\nLooking at the changes in the \"__post_init__\" function, it can be seen that the function has been modified to include a call to \"self._validate_default(default=self.default)\" when a default value is present. This change is likely part of the effort to validate default property values, as mentioned in the commit message.\n\nGiven this, it can be inferred that the \"__post_init__\" function is relevant to the vulnerability that the commit is trying to fix, and it has been changed in response to this vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "snake_case", "code_before": "def snake_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.snakecase(group_title(_sanitize(value)))\n", "code_after": "def snake_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.snakecase(group_title(sanitize(value))))\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 234106368441343, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "_sanitize", "code_before": "def _sanitize(value: str) -> str:\n    return re.sub(r\"[^\\w _-]+\", \"\", value)\n", "code_after": ""}, {"function_name": "group_title", "code_before": "def group_title(value: str) -> str:\n    value = re.sub(r\"([A-Z]{2,})([A-Z][a-z]|[ -_]|$)\", lambda m: m.group(1).title() + m.group(2), value.strip())\n    value = re.sub(r\"(^|[ _-])([A-Z])\", lambda m: m.group(1) + m.group(2).lower(), value)\n    return value\n", "code_after": "def group_title(value: str) -> str:\n    value = re.sub(r\"([A-Z]{2,})([A-Z][a-z]|[ \\-_]|$)\", lambda m: m.group(1).title() + m.group(2), value.strip())\n    value = re.sub(r\"(^|[ _-])([A-Z])\", lambda m: m.group(1) + m.group(2).lower(), value)\n    return value\n"}, {"function_name": "pascal_case", "code_before": "def pascal_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.pascalcase(_sanitize(value))\n", "code_after": "def pascal_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.pascalcase(sanitize(value)))\n"}, {"function_name": "kebab_case", "code_before": "def kebab_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.spinalcase(group_title(_sanitize(value)))\n", "code_after": "def kebab_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.spinalcase(group_title(sanitize(value))))\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `snake_case` was changed as part of a commit that aimed to address a vulnerability related to arbitrary code execution. The vulnerability was due to the generation of arbitrary Python code from a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document.\n\nThe change in the `snake_case` function is the addition of the `fix_keywords` function call. This change is part of a larger set of changes that aim to sanitize and validate the inputs used to generate Python code. \n\nGiven the context, it seems that the `snake_case` function was vulnerable to the described issue, as it was directly involved in the generation of Python code and its modification is part of the fix to the vulnerability. Therefore, the answer is 1.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_sanitize", "code_before": "def _sanitize(value: str) -> str:\n    return re.sub(r\"[^\\w _-]+\", \"\", value)\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 234106368441343, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "snake_case", "code_before": "def snake_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.snakecase(group_title(_sanitize(value)))\n", "code_after": "def snake_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.snakecase(group_title(sanitize(value))))\n"}, {"function_name": "group_title", "code_before": "def group_title(value: str) -> str:\n    value = re.sub(r\"([A-Z]{2,})([A-Z][a-z]|[ -_]|$)\", lambda m: m.group(1).title() + m.group(2), value.strip())\n    value = re.sub(r\"(^|[ _-])([A-Z])\", lambda m: m.group(1) + m.group(2).lower(), value)\n    return value\n", "code_after": "def group_title(value: str) -> str:\n    value = re.sub(r\"([A-Z]{2,})([A-Z][a-z]|[ \\-_]|$)\", lambda m: m.group(1).title() + m.group(2), value.strip())\n    value = re.sub(r\"(^|[ _-])([A-Z])\", lambda m: m.group(1) + m.group(2).lower(), value)\n    return value\n"}, {"function_name": "pascal_case", "code_before": "def pascal_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.pascalcase(_sanitize(value))\n", "code_after": "def pascal_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.pascalcase(sanitize(value)))\n"}, {"function_name": "kebab_case", "code_before": "def kebab_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.spinalcase(group_title(_sanitize(value)))\n", "code_after": "def kebab_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.spinalcase(group_title(sanitize(value))))\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the vulnerability in question pertains to the potential for arbitrary Python code execution when clients are generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document. The commit message and the description of the vulnerability suggest that the issue was addressed by sanitizing all strings used as file/directory names and escaping quotation marks in all names/descriptions.\n\nThe function \"_sanitize\" was removed in the commit, and its functionality appears to have been replaced by a new function called \"sanitize\" (as seen in the changes to other functions like \"snake_case\", \"pascal_case\", and \"kebab_case\"). This suggests that the \"_sanitize\" function was indeed part of the vulnerability, as it was responsible for sanitizing input but was not doing so adequately.\n\nTherefore, the \"_sanitize\" function can be considered as vulnerable, and its removal and replacement with a more secure function is relevant to the fix for the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "pascal_case", "code_before": "def pascal_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.pascalcase(_sanitize(value))\n", "code_after": "def pascal_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.pascalcase(sanitize(value)))\n", "commit_message": "Merge pull request from GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f\n\n* All strings used as file/directory names are now sanitized to address the path traversal vulnerabilities\n\n* Switched calls to utils.spinal_case to utils.kebab_case\n\n* Quotation marks are now escaped in all names/descriptions to address arbitrary code execution issues\n\n* Sanitized identifiers are now also checked for keyword collision\n\n* Added validation for default property values where possible\n\nAddress arbitrary code execution vulnerability\n\n* Enum keys are now also sanitized\n\n* Update end_to_end_tests/fastapi_app/__init__.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/parser/properties.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Changed NotImplementedErrors to ValidationErrors\n\n* Updated changelog\n\n* Fixed regex\n\n* Update openapi_python_client/utils.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* Fixed rendering of union, datetime, and date properties. Added e2e test for all defaults\n\n* Fixed rendering of date/datetime default values when timezone is included\n\n* Simplified datetime rendering\n\n* Fixed typo\n\n* Readded support for DictProperty defaults and fixed a bug with json body template rendering\n\n* Fixed dict json body compatibility\n\nCo-authored-by: Ethan Mann <emann@triaxtec.com>\nCo-authored-by: Dylan Anthony <43723790+dbanty@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/openapi-generators/openapi-python-client/commit/f7a56aae32cba823a77a84a1f10400799b19c19a", "description": "### Impact\nClients generated with a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document can generate arbitrary Python code. Subsequent execution of this malicious client is arbitrary code execution.\n\nGiving this a CVSS of 8.0 (high) with CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H/E:P/RL:U/RC:C .\n\n### Patches\nFix will be included in version 0.5.3\n\n### Workarounds\nInspect OpenAPI documents before generating, or inspect generated code before executing.\n\n### For more information\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory:\n* Open an issue in [openapi-python-client](https://github.com/triaxtec/openapi-python-client/issues)\n* Email us at [danthony@triaxtec.com](mailto:danthony@triaxtec.com)", "file_change_id": 234106368441343, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-9x4c-63pf-525f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "snake_case", "code_before": "def snake_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.snakecase(group_title(_sanitize(value)))\n", "code_after": "def snake_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.snakecase(group_title(sanitize(value))))\n"}, {"function_name": "_sanitize", "code_before": "def _sanitize(value: str) -> str:\n    return re.sub(r\"[^\\w _-]+\", \"\", value)\n", "code_after": ""}, {"function_name": "group_title", "code_before": "def group_title(value: str) -> str:\n    value = re.sub(r\"([A-Z]{2,})([A-Z][a-z]|[ -_]|$)\", lambda m: m.group(1).title() + m.group(2), value.strip())\n    value = re.sub(r\"(^|[ _-])([A-Z])\", lambda m: m.group(1) + m.group(2).lower(), value)\n    return value\n", "code_after": "def group_title(value: str) -> str:\n    value = re.sub(r\"([A-Z]{2,})([A-Z][a-z]|[ \\-_]|$)\", lambda m: m.group(1).title() + m.group(2), value.strip())\n    value = re.sub(r\"(^|[ _-])([A-Z])\", lambda m: m.group(1) + m.group(2).lower(), value)\n    return value\n"}, {"function_name": "kebab_case", "code_before": "def kebab_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return stringcase.spinalcase(group_title(_sanitize(value)))\n", "code_after": "def kebab_case(value: str) -> str:\n    return fix_keywords(stringcase.spinalcase(group_title(sanitize(value))))\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described in the advisory is related to arbitrary code execution due to unsanitized inputs from a maliciously crafted OpenAPI Document. The changes in the commit are aimed at sanitizing these inputs to prevent such attacks.\n\nLooking at the changes in the `pascal_case` function, the `_sanitize` function call has been replaced with a `sanitize` function call, and a new `fix_keywords` function is applied to the result. This change is directly related to the vulnerability, as it is part of the effort to sanitize inputs and prevent arbitrary code execution.\n\nThe other functions changed in the commit (`snake_case`, `_sanitize`, `group_title`, `kebab_case`) also show similar changes, replacing `_sanitize` with `sanitize` and applying `fix_keywords`, which further supports the conclusion that these changes are relevant to the vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, the `pascal_case` function is vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "job_buttons", "code_before": "def job_buttons(context, obj):\n    \"\"\"\n    Render all applicable job buttons for the given object.\n    \"\"\"\n    content_type = ContentType.objects.get_for_model(obj)\n    buttons = JobButton.objects.filter(content_types=content_type)\n    if not buttons:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Pass select context data when rendering the JobButton\n    button_context = {\n        \"obj\": obj,\n        \"debug\": context.get(\"debug\", False),  # django.template.context_processors.debug\n        \"request\": context[\"request\"],  # django.template.context_processors.request\n        \"user\": context[\"user\"],  # django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth\n        \"perms\": context[\"perms\"],  # django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth\n    }\n    buttons_html = forms_html = mark_safe(\"\")  # noqa: S308\n    group_names = OrderedDict()\n\n    hidden_inputs = format_html(\n        HIDDEN_INPUTS,\n        csrf_token=context[\"csrf_token\"],\n        object_pk=obj.pk,\n        object_model_name=f\"{content_type.app_label}.{content_type.model}\",\n        redirect_path=context[\"request\"].path,\n    )\n\n    for jb in buttons:\n        template_args = {\n            \"button_id\": jb.pk,\n            \"button_text\": jb.text,\n            \"button_class\": jb.button_class,\n            \"button_url\": reverse(\"extras:jobbutton_run\", kwargs={\"pk\": jb.pk}),\n            \"object\": obj,\n            \"job\": jb.job,\n            \"hidden_inputs\": hidden_inputs,\n            \"disabled\": \"\" if context[\"user\"].has_perms((\"extras.run_jobbutton\", \"extras.run_job\")) else \"disabled\",\n        }\n\n        # Organize job buttons by group\n        if jb.group_name:\n            group_names.setdefault(jb.group_name, [])\n            group_names[jb.group_name].append(jb)\n\n        # Add non-grouped buttons\n        else:\n            try:\n                text_rendered = render_jinja2(jb.text, button_context)\n                if text_rendered:\n                    template_args[\"button_text\"] = text_rendered\n                    if jb.confirmation:\n                        buttons_html += format_html(CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                        forms_html += format_html(CONFIRM_MODAL, **template_args)\n                    else:\n                        buttons_html += format_html(NO_CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                        forms_html += format_html(NO_CONFIRM_FORM, **template_args)\n            except Exception as e:\n                buttons_html += format_html(\n                    '<a class=\"btn btn-sm btn-default\" disabled=\"disabled\" title=\"{}\">'\n                    '<i class=\"mdi mdi-alert\"></i> {}</a>\\n',\n                    e,\n                    jb.name,\n                )\n\n    # Add grouped buttons to template\n    for group_name, buttons in group_names.items():\n        group_button_class = buttons[0].button_class\n\n        buttons_rendered = mark_safe(\"\")  # noqa: S308\n\n        for jb in buttons:\n            template_args = {\n                \"button_id\": jb.pk,\n                \"button_text\": jb.text,\n                \"button_class\": \"link\",\n                \"button_url\": reverse(\"extras:jobbutton_run\", kwargs={\"pk\": jb.pk}),\n                \"object\": obj,\n                \"job\": jb.job,\n                \"hidden_inputs\": hidden_inputs,\n                \"disabled\": \"\" if context[\"user\"].has_perms((\"extras.run_jobbutton\", \"extras.run_job\")) else \"disabled\",\n            }\n            try:\n                text_rendered = render_jinja2(jb.text, button_context)\n                if text_rendered:\n                    template_args[\"button_text\"] = text_rendered\n                    if jb.confirmation:\n                        buttons_rendered += (\n                            mark_safe(\"<li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                            + format_html(CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                            + mark_safe(\"</li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                        )\n                        forms_html += format_html(CONFIRM_MODAL, **template_args)\n                    else:\n                        buttons_rendered += (\n                            mark_safe(\"<li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                            + format_html(NO_CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                            + mark_safe(\"</li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                        )\n                        forms_html += format_html(NO_CONFIRM_FORM, **template_args)\n            except Exception as e:\n                buttons_rendered += format_html(\n                    '<li><a disabled=\"disabled\" title=\"{}\"><span class=\"text-muted\">'\n                    '<i class=\"mdi mdi-alert\"></i> {}</span></a></li>',\n                    e,\n                    jb.name,\n                )\n\n        if buttons_rendered:\n            buttons_html += format_html(\n                GROUP_DROPDOWN,\n                group_button_class=group_button_class,\n                group_name=group_name,\n                grouped_buttons=buttons_rendered,\n            )\n\n    # We want all of the buttons first and then any modals and forms so the buttons render properly\n    return buttons_html + forms_html\n", "code_after": "def job_buttons(context, obj):\n    \"\"\"\n    Render all applicable job buttons for the given object.\n    \"\"\"\n    content_type = ContentType.objects.get_for_model(obj)\n    # We will enforce \"run\" permission later in deciding which buttons to show as disabled.\n    buttons = JobButton.objects.filter(content_types=content_type)\n    if not buttons:\n        return SAFE_EMPTY_STR\n\n    buttons_html = forms_html = SAFE_EMPTY_STR\n    group_names = OrderedDict()\n\n    for jb in buttons:\n        # Organize job buttons by group for later processing\n        if jb.group_name:\n            group_names.setdefault(jb.group_name, []).append(jb)\n\n        # Render and add non-grouped buttons\n        else:\n            button_html, form_html = _render_job_button_for_obj(jb, obj, context, content_type)\n            buttons_html += button_html\n            forms_html += form_html\n\n    # Add grouped buttons to template\n    for group_name, buttons in group_names.items():\n        group_button_class = buttons[0].button_class\n\n        buttons_rendered = SAFE_EMPTY_STR\n\n        for jb in buttons:\n            # Render grouped buttons as list items\n            button_html, form_html = _render_job_button_for_obj(jb, obj, context, content_type)\n            buttons_rendered += format_html(\"<li>{}</li>\", button_html)\n            forms_html += form_html\n\n        if buttons_rendered:\n            buttons_html += format_html(\n                GROUP_DROPDOWN,\n                group_button_class=group_button_class,\n                group_name=group_name,\n                grouped_buttons=buttons_rendered,\n            )\n\n    # We want all of the buttons first and then any modals and forms so the buttons render properly\n    return buttons_html + forms_html\n", "commit_message": "Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons (#4993)\n\n* Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\r\n\r\n* s/SAFE_EMPTY/SAFE_EMPTY_STR/\r\n\r\n* Improve return_url handling\r\n\r\n---------\r\n\r\nCo-authored-by: Gary Snider <75227981+gsnider2195@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/commit/3d964f996f4926126c1d7853ca87b2ff475997a2", "description": "### Impact\n\nWhen submitting a Job to run via a Job Button, only the model-level `extras.run_job` permission is checked (i.e., does the user have permission to run Jobs in general?). Object-level permissions (i.e., does the user have permission to run this *specific* Job?) are not enforced by the URL/view used in this case (`/extras/job-button/<uuid>/run/`) The effect is that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\n> Not all Jobs can be configured as JobButtons; only those implemented as subclasses of `JobButtonReceiver` can be used in this way, so this vulnerability only applies specifically to `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses.\n\nAdditionally, although the documentation states that both `extras.run_job` permission and `extras.run_jobbutton` permission must be granted to a user in order to run Jobs via JobButton, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. Furthermore, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission never prevented invoking Jobs (including `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses) via the normal \"Job Run\" UI, so after some discussion, we've decided that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is redundant, and as it never achieved its stated/documented purpose, the fixes below will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\n### Patches\n_Has the problem been patched? What versions should users upgrade to?_\n\nFix will be available in Nautobot 1.6.8 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995) and 2.1.0 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993)\n\n### Workarounds\n_Is there a way for users to fix or remediate the vulnerability without upgrading?_\n\nPartial mitigation can be achieved by auditing `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses defined in the system and restricting which users are permitted to create or edit JobButton records. \n\n### References\n\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/issues/4988\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995\n", "file_change_id": 256269513889694, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vf5m-xrhm-v999", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `job_buttons` has been modified to address a security vulnerability. The vulnerability allowed a user with permissions to run even a single Job to actually run all configured JobButton Jobs. \n\nThe changes in the function `job_buttons` are mainly refactoring and the introduction of a new function `_render_job_button_for_obj`. This new function is likely responsible for the enforcement of object-level permissions, which was the main issue in the vulnerability. \n\nTherefore, it can be concluded that the function `job_buttons` was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "post", "code_before": "    def post(self, request, pk):\n        post_data = request.POST\n        job_button = JobButton.objects.get(pk=pk)\n        job_model = job_button.job\n        result = JobResult.enqueue_job(\n            job_model=job_model,\n            user=request.user,\n            object_pk=post_data[\"object_pk\"],\n            object_model_name=post_data[\"object_model_name\"],\n        )\n        msg = format_html('Job enqueued. <a href=\"{}\">Click here for the results.</a>', result.get_absolute_url())\n        messages.info(request=request, message=msg)\n        return redirect(post_data[\"redirect_path\"])\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons (#4993)\n\n* Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\r\n\r\n* s/SAFE_EMPTY/SAFE_EMPTY_STR/\r\n\r\n* Improve return_url handling\r\n\r\n---------\r\n\r\nCo-authored-by: Gary Snider <75227981+gsnider2195@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/commit/3d964f996f4926126c1d7853ca87b2ff475997a2", "description": "### Impact\n\nWhen submitting a Job to run via a Job Button, only the model-level `extras.run_job` permission is checked (i.e., does the user have permission to run Jobs in general?). Object-level permissions (i.e., does the user have permission to run this *specific* Job?) are not enforced by the URL/view used in this case (`/extras/job-button/<uuid>/run/`) The effect is that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\n> Not all Jobs can be configured as JobButtons; only those implemented as subclasses of `JobButtonReceiver` can be used in this way, so this vulnerability only applies specifically to `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses.\n\nAdditionally, although the documentation states that both `extras.run_job` permission and `extras.run_jobbutton` permission must be granted to a user in order to run Jobs via JobButton, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. Furthermore, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission never prevented invoking Jobs (including `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses) via the normal \"Job Run\" UI, so after some discussion, we've decided that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is redundant, and as it never achieved its stated/documented purpose, the fixes below will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\n### Patches\n_Has the problem been patched? What versions should users upgrade to?_\n\nFix will be available in Nautobot 1.6.8 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995) and 2.1.0 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993)\n\n### Workarounds\n_Is there a way for users to fix or remediate the vulnerability without upgrading?_\n\nPartial mitigation can be achieved by auditing `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses defined in the system and restricting which users are permitted to create or edit JobButton records. \n\n### References\n\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/issues/4988\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995\n", "file_change_id": 85903319968456, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vf5m-xrhm-v999", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "get_required_permission", "code_before": "    def get_required_permission(self):\n        return \"extras.run_job\"\n", "code_after": ""}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the vulnerability lies in the lack of object-level permissions enforcement when submitting a Job to run via a Job Button. This means that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\nThe function `post` is removed in the commit, which was responsible for enqueuing jobs. This function was not checking for object-level permissions, which is the core of the vulnerability. Therefore, it can be inferred that this function was vulnerable.\n\nThe commit message and the description of the vulnerability also support this conclusion. The commit message mentions \"Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\", which directly relates to the vulnerability described.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1, indicating that the function is vulnerable.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "get_required_permission", "code_before": "    def get_required_permission(self):\n        return \"extras.run_job\"\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons (#4993)\n\n* Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\r\n\r\n* s/SAFE_EMPTY/SAFE_EMPTY_STR/\r\n\r\n* Improve return_url handling\r\n\r\n---------\r\n\r\nCo-authored-by: Gary Snider <75227981+gsnider2195@users.noreply.github.com>", "commit": "https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/commit/3d964f996f4926126c1d7853ca87b2ff475997a2", "description": "### Impact\n\nWhen submitting a Job to run via a Job Button, only the model-level `extras.run_job` permission is checked (i.e., does the user have permission to run Jobs in general?). Object-level permissions (i.e., does the user have permission to run this *specific* Job?) are not enforced by the URL/view used in this case (`/extras/job-button/<uuid>/run/`) The effect is that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\n> Not all Jobs can be configured as JobButtons; only those implemented as subclasses of `JobButtonReceiver` can be used in this way, so this vulnerability only applies specifically to `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses.\n\nAdditionally, although the documentation states that both `extras.run_job` permission and `extras.run_jobbutton` permission must be granted to a user in order to run Jobs via JobButton, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. Furthermore, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission never prevented invoking Jobs (including `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses) via the normal \"Job Run\" UI, so after some discussion, we've decided that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is redundant, and as it never achieved its stated/documented purpose, the fixes below will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\n### Patches\n_Has the problem been patched? What versions should users upgrade to?_\n\nFix will be available in Nautobot 1.6.8 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995) and 2.1.0 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993)\n\n### Workarounds\n_Is there a way for users to fix or remediate the vulnerability without upgrading?_\n\nPartial mitigation can be achieved by auditing `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses defined in the system and restricting which users are permitted to create or edit JobButton records. \n\n### References\n\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/issues/4988\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995\n", "file_change_id": 85903319968456, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vf5m-xrhm-v999", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "post", "code_before": "    def post(self, request, pk):\n        post_data = request.POST\n        job_button = JobButton.objects.get(pk=pk)\n        job_model = job_button.job\n        result = JobResult.enqueue_job(\n            job_model=job_model,\n            user=request.user,\n            object_pk=post_data[\"object_pk\"],\n            object_model_name=post_data[\"object_model_name\"],\n        )\n        msg = format_html('Job enqueued. <a href=\"{}\">Click here for the results.</a>', result.get_absolute_url())\n        messages.info(request=request, message=msg)\n        return redirect(post_data[\"redirect_path\"])\n", "code_after": ""}, {"function_name": "post", "code_before": "    def post(self, request, class_path=None, pk=None):\n        job_model = self._get_job_model_or_404(class_path, pk)\n\n        job_form = job_model.job_class.as_form(request.POST, request.FILES) if job_model.job_class is not None else None\n        schedule_form = forms.JobScheduleForm(request.POST)\n        task_queue = request.POST.get(\"_task_queue\")\n\n        # Allow execution only if a worker process is running and the job is runnable.\n        if not get_worker_count(queue=task_queue):\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to run or schedule job: Celery worker process not running.\")\n        elif not job_model.installed or job_model.job_class is None:\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to run or schedule job: Job is not presently installed.\")\n        elif not job_model.enabled:\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to run or schedule job: Job is not enabled to be run.\")\n        elif job_model.has_sensitive_variables and request.POST[\"_schedule_type\"] != JobExecutionType.TYPE_IMMEDIATELY:\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to schedule job: Job may have sensitive input variables.\")\n        elif job_model.has_sensitive_variables and job_model.approval_required:\n            messages.error(\n                request,\n                \"Unable to run or schedule job: \"\n                \"This job is flagged as possibly having sensitive variables but is also flagged as requiring approval.\"\n                \"One of these two flags must be removed before this job can be scheduled or run.\",\n            )\n        elif job_form is not None and job_form.is_valid() and schedule_form.is_valid():\n            task_queue = job_form.cleaned_data.pop(\"_task_queue\", None)\n            dryrun = job_form.cleaned_data.get(\"dryrun\", False)\n            # Run the job. A new JobResult is created.\n            profile = job_form.cleaned_data.pop(\"_profile\")\n            schedule_type = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_schedule_type\"]\n\n            if (not dryrun and job_model.approval_required) or schedule_type in JobExecutionType.SCHEDULE_CHOICES:\n                crontab = \"\"\n\n                if schedule_type == JobExecutionType.TYPE_IMMEDIATELY:\n                    # The job must be approved.\n                    # If the schedule_type is immediate, we still create the task, but mark it for approval\n                    # as a once in the future task with the due date set to the current time. This means\n                    # when approval is granted, the task is immediately due for execution.\n                    schedule_type = JobExecutionType.TYPE_FUTURE\n                    schedule_datetime = timezone.now()\n                    schedule_name = f\"{job_model} - {schedule_datetime}\"\n\n                else:\n                    schedule_name = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_schedule_name\"]\n\n                    if schedule_type == JobExecutionType.TYPE_CUSTOM:\n                        crontab = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_recurrence_custom_time\"]\n                        # doing .get(\"key\", \"default\") returns None instead of \"default\" here for some reason\n                        schedule_datetime = schedule_form.cleaned_data.get(\"_schedule_start_time\")\n                        if schedule_datetime is None:\n                            # \"_schedule_start_time\" is checked against ScheduledJob.earliest_possible_time()\n                            # which returns timezone.now() + timedelta(seconds=15)\n                            schedule_datetime = timezone.now() + timedelta(seconds=20)\n                    else:\n                        schedule_datetime = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_schedule_start_time\"]\n\n                celery_kwargs = {\"nautobot_job_profile\": profile, \"queue\": task_queue}\n                scheduled_job = ScheduledJob(\n                    name=schedule_name,\n                    task=job_model.job_class.registered_name,\n                    job_model=job_model,\n                    start_time=schedule_datetime,\n                    description=f\"Nautobot job {schedule_name} scheduled by {request.user} for {schedule_datetime}\",\n                    kwargs=job_model.job_class.serialize_data(job_form.cleaned_data),\n                    celery_kwargs=celery_kwargs,\n                    interval=schedule_type,\n                    one_off=schedule_type == JobExecutionType.TYPE_FUTURE,\n                    queue=task_queue,\n                    user=request.user,\n                    approval_required=job_model.approval_required,\n                    crontab=crontab,\n                )\n                scheduled_job.validated_save()\n\n                if job_model.approval_required:\n                    messages.success(request, f\"Job {schedule_name} successfully submitted for approval\")\n                    return redirect(\"extras:scheduledjob_approval_queue_list\")\n                else:\n                    messages.success(request, f\"Job {schedule_name} successfully scheduled\")\n                    return redirect(\"extras:scheduledjob_list\")\n\n            else:\n                # Enqueue job for immediate execution\n                job_kwargs = job_model.job_class.prepare_job_kwargs(job_form.cleaned_data)\n                job_result = JobResult.enqueue_job(\n                    job_model,\n                    request.user,\n                    profile=profile,\n                    task_queue=task_queue,\n                    **job_model.job_class.serialize_data(job_kwargs),\n                )\n\n                return redirect(\"extras:jobresult\", pk=job_result.pk)\n\n        template_name = \"extras/job.html\"\n        if job_model.job_class is not None and hasattr(job_model.job_class, \"template_name\"):\n            try:\n                get_template(job_model.job_class.template_name)\n                template_name = job_model.job_class.template_name\n            except TemplateDoesNotExist as err:\n                messages.error(request, f'Unable to render requested custom job template \"{template_name}\": {err}')\n\n        return render(\n            request,\n            template_name,\n            {\n                \"job_model\": job_model,\n                \"job_form\": job_form,\n                \"schedule_form\": schedule_form,\n            },\n        )\n", "code_after": "    def post(self, request, class_path=None, pk=None):\n        job_model = self._get_job_model_or_404(class_path, pk)\n\n        job_form = job_model.job_class.as_form(request.POST, request.FILES) if job_model.job_class is not None else None\n        schedule_form = forms.JobScheduleForm(request.POST)\n        task_queue = request.POST.get(\"_task_queue\")\n\n        return_url = request.POST.get(\"_return_url\")\n        if return_url is not None and url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme(url=return_url, allowed_hosts=request.get_host()):\n            return_url = iri_to_uri(return_url)\n        else:\n            return_url = None\n\n        # Allow execution only if a worker process is running and the job is runnable.\n        if not get_worker_count(queue=task_queue):\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to run or schedule job: Celery worker process not running.\")\n        elif not job_model.installed or job_model.job_class is None:\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to run or schedule job: Job is not presently installed.\")\n        elif not job_model.enabled:\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to run or schedule job: Job is not enabled to be run.\")\n        elif (\n            job_model.has_sensitive_variables\n            and request.POST.get(\"_schedule_type\") != JobExecutionType.TYPE_IMMEDIATELY\n        ):\n            messages.error(request, \"Unable to schedule job: Job may have sensitive input variables.\")\n        elif job_model.has_sensitive_variables and job_model.approval_required:\n            messages.error(\n                request,\n                \"Unable to run or schedule job: \"\n                \"This job is flagged as possibly having sensitive variables but is also flagged as requiring approval.\"\n                \"One of these two flags must be removed before this job can be scheduled or run.\",\n            )\n        elif job_form is not None and job_form.is_valid() and schedule_form.is_valid():\n            task_queue = job_form.cleaned_data.pop(\"_task_queue\", None)\n            dryrun = job_form.cleaned_data.get(\"dryrun\", False)\n            # Run the job. A new JobResult is created.\n            profile = job_form.cleaned_data.pop(\"_profile\")\n            schedule_type = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_schedule_type\"]\n\n            if (not dryrun and job_model.approval_required) or schedule_type in JobExecutionType.SCHEDULE_CHOICES:\n                crontab = \"\"\n\n                if schedule_type == JobExecutionType.TYPE_IMMEDIATELY:\n                    # The job must be approved.\n                    # If the schedule_type is immediate, we still create the task, but mark it for approval\n                    # as a once in the future task with the due date set to the current time. This means\n                    # when approval is granted, the task is immediately due for execution.\n                    schedule_type = JobExecutionType.TYPE_FUTURE\n                    schedule_datetime = timezone.now()\n                    schedule_name = f\"{job_model} - {schedule_datetime}\"\n\n                else:\n                    schedule_name = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_schedule_name\"]\n\n                    if schedule_type == JobExecutionType.TYPE_CUSTOM:\n                        crontab = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_recurrence_custom_time\"]\n                        # doing .get(\"key\", \"default\") returns None instead of \"default\" here for some reason\n                        schedule_datetime = schedule_form.cleaned_data.get(\"_schedule_start_time\")\n                        if schedule_datetime is None:\n                            # \"_schedule_start_time\" is checked against ScheduledJob.earliest_possible_time()\n                            # which returns timezone.now() + timedelta(seconds=15)\n                            schedule_datetime = timezone.now() + timedelta(seconds=20)\n                    else:\n                        schedule_datetime = schedule_form.cleaned_data[\"_schedule_start_time\"]\n\n                celery_kwargs = {\"nautobot_job_profile\": profile, \"queue\": task_queue}\n                scheduled_job = ScheduledJob(\n                    name=schedule_name,\n                    task=job_model.job_class.registered_name,\n                    job_model=job_model,\n                    start_time=schedule_datetime,\n                    description=f\"Nautobot job {schedule_name} scheduled by {request.user} for {schedule_datetime}\",\n                    kwargs=job_model.job_class.serialize_data(job_form.cleaned_data),\n                    celery_kwargs=celery_kwargs,\n                    interval=schedule_type,\n                    one_off=schedule_type == JobExecutionType.TYPE_FUTURE,\n                    queue=task_queue,\n                    user=request.user,\n                    approval_required=job_model.approval_required,\n                    crontab=crontab,\n                )\n                scheduled_job.validated_save()\n\n                if job_model.approval_required:\n                    messages.success(request, f\"Job {schedule_name} successfully submitted for approval\")\n                    return redirect(return_url if return_url else \"extras:scheduledjob_approval_queue_list\")\n                else:\n                    messages.success(request, f\"Job {schedule_name} successfully scheduled\")\n                    return redirect(return_url if return_url else \"extras:scheduledjob_list\")\n\n            else:\n                # Enqueue job for immediate execution\n                job_kwargs = job_model.job_class.prepare_job_kwargs(job_form.cleaned_data)\n                job_result = JobResult.enqueue_job(\n                    job_model,\n                    request.user,\n                    profile=profile,\n                    task_queue=task_queue,\n                    **job_model.job_class.serialize_data(job_kwargs),\n                )\n\n                if return_url:\n                    messages.info(\n                        request,\n                        format_html(\n                            'Job enqueued. <a href=\"{}\">Click here for the results.</a>',\n                            job_result.get_absolute_url(),\n                        ),\n                    )\n                    return redirect(return_url)\n\n                return redirect(\"extras:jobresult\", pk=job_result.pk)\n\n        if return_url:\n            return redirect(return_url)\n\n        template_name = \"extras/job.html\"\n        if job_model.job_class is not None and hasattr(job_model.job_class, \"template_name\"):\n            try:\n                get_template(job_model.job_class.template_name)\n                template_name = job_model.job_class.template_name\n            except TemplateDoesNotExist as err:\n                messages.error(request, f'Unable to render requested custom job template \"{template_name}\": {err}')\n\n        return render(\n            request,\n            template_name,\n            {\n                \"job_model\": job_model,\n                \"job_form\": job_form,\n                \"schedule_form\": schedule_form,\n            },\n        )\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described in the advisory is related to the lack of enforcement of object-level permissions when submitting a Job to run via a Job Button. This means that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\nThe function `get_required_permission` is removed in the commit. This function was returning a string \"extras.run_job\", which seems to be a permission level. The removal of this function might be related to the changes in the permission system described in the advisory.\n\nHowever, the advisory also mentions that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. The fixes will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\nGiven this information, it seems that the `get_required_permission` function is related to the vulnerability, as it is part of the permission system that is being changed to fix the vulnerability. Therefore, the function can be considered as vulnerable.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "receive_job_button", "code_before": "    def receive_job_button(self, obj):\n        user = self.request.user\n        if isinstance(obj, Site):\n            if not user.has_perm(\"dcim.add_site\"):\n                self.log_failure(obj=obj, message=f\"User '{user}' does not have permission to add a Site.\")\n            else:\n                self._run_site_job(obj)\n        if isinstance(obj, Device):\n            if not user.has_perm(\"dcim.add_device\"):\n                self.log_failure(obj=obj, message=f\"User '{user}' does not have permission to add a Device.\")\n            else:\n                self._run_device_job(obj)\n        self.log_failure(obj=obj, message=f\"Unable to run Job Button for type {type(obj).__name__}.\")\n", "code_after": "    def receive_job_button(self, obj):\n        user = self.request.user\n        if isinstance(obj, Site):\n            if not user.has_perm(\"dcim.add_site\"):\n                self.log_failure(obj=obj, message=f\"User '{user}' does not have permission to add a Site.\")\n            else:\n                self._run_site_job(obj)\n        elif isinstance(obj, Device):\n            if not user.has_perm(\"dcim.add_device\"):\n                self.log_failure(obj=obj, message=f\"User '{user}' does not have permission to add a Device.\")\n            else:\n                self._run_device_job(obj)\n        else:\n            self.log_failure(obj=obj, message=f\"Unable to run Job Button for type {type(obj).__name__}.\")\n", "commit_message": "[1.6] Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons (#4995)\n\n* Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\r\n\r\n* Add imports\r\n\r\n* s/SAFE_EMPTY/SAFE_EMPTY_STR/\r\n\r\n* Improve return_url handling", "commit": "https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/commit/d33d0c15a36948c45244e5b5e10bc79b8e62de7f", "description": "### Impact\n\nWhen submitting a Job to run via a Job Button, only the model-level `extras.run_job` permission is checked (i.e., does the user have permission to run Jobs in general?). Object-level permissions (i.e., does the user have permission to run this *specific* Job?) are not enforced by the URL/view used in this case (`/extras/job-button/<uuid>/run/`) The effect is that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\n> Not all Jobs can be configured as JobButtons; only those implemented as subclasses of `JobButtonReceiver` can be used in this way, so this vulnerability only applies specifically to `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses.\n\nAdditionally, although the documentation states that both `extras.run_job` permission and `extras.run_jobbutton` permission must be granted to a user in order to run Jobs via JobButton, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. Furthermore, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission never prevented invoking Jobs (including `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses) via the normal \"Job Run\" UI, so after some discussion, we've decided that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is redundant, and as it never achieved its stated/documented purpose, the fixes below will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\n### Patches\n_Has the problem been patched? What versions should users upgrade to?_\n\nFix will be available in Nautobot 1.6.8 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995) and 2.1.0 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993)\n\n### Workarounds\n_Is there a way for users to fix or remediate the vulnerability without upgrading?_\n\nPartial mitigation can be achieved by auditing `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses defined in the system and restricting which users are permitted to create or edit JobButton records. \n\n### References\n\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/issues/4988\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995\n", "file_change_id": 64826152276233, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vf5m-xrhm-v999", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function \"receive_job_button\" has been modified to fix a security vulnerability. The vulnerability was that a user with permissions to run even a single Job could actually run all configured JobButton Jobs. \n\nThe change in the function is a minor one, where the second \"if\" condition is changed to \"elif\". This change ensures that the function checks if the object is a \"Site\" or a \"Device\" and doesn't log a failure if the object is either of these two. \n\nThis change is directly related to the vulnerability as it is part of the permission checks that are performed when a job is run. Therefore, it can be concluded that the function was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "job_buttons", "code_before": "def job_buttons(context, obj):\n    \"\"\"\n    Render all applicable job buttons for the given object.\n    \"\"\"\n    content_type = ContentType.objects.get_for_model(obj)\n    buttons = JobButton.objects.filter(content_types=content_type)\n    if not buttons:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Pass select context data when rendering the JobButton\n    button_context = {\n        \"obj\": obj,\n        \"debug\": context.get(\"debug\", False),  # django.template.context_processors.debug\n        \"request\": context[\"request\"],  # django.template.context_processors.request\n        \"user\": context[\"user\"],  # django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth\n        \"perms\": context[\"perms\"],  # django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth\n    }\n    buttons_html = forms_html = mark_safe(\"\")  # noqa: S308\n    group_names = OrderedDict()\n\n    hidden_inputs = format_html(\n        HIDDEN_INPUTS,\n        csrf_token=context[\"csrf_token\"],\n        object_pk=obj.pk,\n        object_model_name=f\"{content_type.app_label}.{content_type.model}\",\n        redirect_path=context[\"request\"].path,\n    )\n\n    for jb in buttons:\n        template_args = {\n            \"button_id\": jb.pk,\n            \"button_text\": jb.text,\n            \"button_class\": jb.button_class,\n            \"button_url\": reverse(\"extras:jobbutton_run\", kwargs={\"pk\": jb.pk}),\n            \"object\": obj,\n            \"job\": jb.job,\n            \"hidden_inputs\": hidden_inputs,\n            \"disabled\": \"\" if context[\"user\"].has_perms((\"extras.run_jobbutton\", \"extras.run_job\")) else \"disabled\",\n        }\n\n        # Organize job buttons by group\n        if jb.group_name:\n            group_names.setdefault(jb.group_name, [])\n            group_names[jb.group_name].append(jb)\n\n        # Add non-grouped buttons\n        else:\n            try:\n                text_rendered = render_jinja2(jb.text, button_context)\n                if text_rendered:\n                    template_args[\"button_text\"] = text_rendered\n                    if jb.confirmation:\n                        buttons_html += format_html(CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                        forms_html += format_html(CONFIRM_MODAL, **template_args)\n                    else:\n                        buttons_html += format_html(NO_CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                        forms_html += format_html(NO_CONFIRM_FORM, **template_args)\n            except Exception as e:\n                buttons_html += format_html(\n                    '<a class=\"btn btn-sm btn-default\" disabled=\"disabled\" title=\"{}\">'\n                    '<i class=\"mdi mdi-alert\"></i> {}</a>\\n',\n                    e,\n                    jb.name,\n                )\n\n    # Add grouped buttons to template\n    for group_name, buttons in group_names.items():\n        group_button_class = buttons[0].button_class\n\n        buttons_rendered = mark_safe(\"\")  # noqa: S308\n\n        for jb in buttons:\n            template_args = {\n                \"button_id\": jb.pk,\n                \"button_text\": jb.text,\n                \"button_class\": \"link\",\n                \"button_url\": reverse(\"extras:jobbutton_run\", kwargs={\"pk\": jb.pk}),\n                \"object\": obj,\n                \"job\": jb.job,\n                \"hidden_inputs\": hidden_inputs,\n                \"disabled\": \"\" if context[\"user\"].has_perms((\"extras.run_jobbutton\", \"extras.run_job\")) else \"disabled\",\n            }\n            try:\n                text_rendered = render_jinja2(jb.text, button_context)\n                if text_rendered:\n                    template_args[\"button_text\"] = text_rendered\n                    if jb.confirmation:\n                        buttons_rendered += (\n                            mark_safe(\"<li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                            + format_html(CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                            + mark_safe(\"</li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                        )\n                        forms_html += format_html(CONFIRM_MODAL, **template_args)\n                    else:\n                        buttons_rendered += (\n                            mark_safe(\"<li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                            + format_html(NO_CONFIRM_BUTTON, **template_args)\n                            + mark_safe(\"</li>\")  # noqa: S308\n                        )\n                        forms_html += format_html(NO_CONFIRM_FORM, **template_args)\n            except Exception as e:\n                buttons_rendered += format_html(\n                    '<li><a disabled=\"disabled\" title=\"{}\"><span class=\"text-muted\">'\n                    '<i class=\"mdi mdi-alert\"></i> {}</span></a></li>',\n                    e,\n                    jb.name,\n                )\n\n        if buttons_rendered:\n            buttons_html += format_html(\n                GROUP_DROPDOWN,\n                group_button_class=group_button_class,\n                group_name=group_name,\n                grouped_buttons=buttons_rendered,\n            )\n\n    # We want all of the buttons first and then any modals and forms so the buttons render properly\n    return buttons_html + forms_html\n", "code_after": "def job_buttons(context, obj):\n    \"\"\"\n    Render all applicable job buttons for the given object.\n    \"\"\"\n    content_type = ContentType.objects.get_for_model(obj)\n    # We will enforce \"run\" permission later in deciding which buttons to show as disabled.\n    buttons = JobButton.objects.filter(content_types=content_type)\n    if not buttons:\n        return SAFE_EMPTY_STR\n\n    buttons_html = forms_html = SAFE_EMPTY_STR\n    group_names = OrderedDict()\n\n    for jb in buttons:\n        # Organize job buttons by group for later processing\n        if jb.group_name:\n            group_names.setdefault(jb.group_name, []).append(jb)\n\n        # Render and add non-grouped buttons\n        else:\n            button_html, form_html = _render_job_button_for_obj(jb, obj, context, content_type)\n            buttons_html += button_html\n            forms_html += form_html\n\n    # Add grouped buttons to template\n    for group_name, buttons in group_names.items():\n        group_button_class = buttons[0].button_class\n\n        buttons_rendered = SAFE_EMPTY_STR\n\n        for jb in buttons:\n            # Render grouped buttons as list items\n            button_html, form_html = _render_job_button_for_obj(jb, obj, context, content_type)\n            buttons_rendered += format_html(\"<li>{}</li>\", button_html)\n            forms_html += form_html\n\n        if buttons_rendered:\n            buttons_html += format_html(\n                GROUP_DROPDOWN,\n                group_button_class=group_button_class,\n                group_name=group_name,\n                grouped_buttons=buttons_rendered,\n            )\n\n    # We want all of the buttons first and then any modals and forms so the buttons render properly\n    return buttons_html + forms_html\n", "commit_message": "[1.6] Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons (#4995)\n\n* Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\r\n\r\n* Add imports\r\n\r\n* s/SAFE_EMPTY/SAFE_EMPTY_STR/\r\n\r\n* Improve return_url handling", "commit": "https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/commit/d33d0c15a36948c45244e5b5e10bc79b8e62de7f", "description": "### Impact\n\nWhen submitting a Job to run via a Job Button, only the model-level `extras.run_job` permission is checked (i.e., does the user have permission to run Jobs in general?). Object-level permissions (i.e., does the user have permission to run this *specific* Job?) are not enforced by the URL/view used in this case (`/extras/job-button/<uuid>/run/`) The effect is that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\n> Not all Jobs can be configured as JobButtons; only those implemented as subclasses of `JobButtonReceiver` can be used in this way, so this vulnerability only applies specifically to `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses.\n\nAdditionally, although the documentation states that both `extras.run_job` permission and `extras.run_jobbutton` permission must be granted to a user in order to run Jobs via JobButton, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. Furthermore, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission never prevented invoking Jobs (including `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses) via the normal \"Job Run\" UI, so after some discussion, we've decided that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is redundant, and as it never achieved its stated/documented purpose, the fixes below will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\n### Patches\n_Has the problem been patched? What versions should users upgrade to?_\n\nFix will be available in Nautobot 1.6.8 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995) and 2.1.0 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993)\n\n### Workarounds\n_Is there a way for users to fix or remediate the vulnerability without upgrading?_\n\nPartial mitigation can be achieved by auditing `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses defined in the system and restricting which users are permitted to create or edit JobButton records. \n\n### References\n\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/issues/4988\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995\n", "file_change_id": 55351503953542, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vf5m-xrhm-v999", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The function \"job_buttons\" is being changed in a commit that is designed to fix a security vulnerability. The vulnerability is related to permissions enforcement on Job Buttons, where a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\nIn the \"job_buttons\" function, the changes are mainly refactoring and the addition of a new function \"_render_job_button_for_obj\". This new function is likely where the actual permission checks are being enforced.\n\nGiven the description of the vulnerability and the changes made to the function, it seems that the \"job_buttons\" function is vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\nSo, the answer is:\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "post", "code_before": "    def post(self, request, pk):\n        post_data = request.POST\n        job_button = JobButton.objects.get(pk=pk)\n        job_model = job_button.job\n        result = JobResult.enqueue_job(\n            func=run_job,\n            name=job_model.class_path,\n            obj_type=get_job_content_type(),\n            user=request.user,\n            data={\n                \"object_pk\": post_data[\"object_pk\"],\n                \"object_model_name\": post_data[\"object_model_name\"],\n            },\n            request=copy_safe_request(request),\n            commit=True,\n        )\n        msg = format_html('Job enqueued. <a href=\"{}\">Click here for the results.</a>', result.get_absolute_url())\n        messages.info(request=request, message=msg)\n        return redirect(post_data[\"redirect_path\"])\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "[1.6] Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons (#4995)\n\n* Fix object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons\r\n\r\n* Add imports\r\n\r\n* s/SAFE_EMPTY/SAFE_EMPTY_STR/\r\n\r\n* Improve return_url handling", "commit": "https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/commit/d33d0c15a36948c45244e5b5e10bc79b8e62de7f", "description": "### Impact\n\nWhen submitting a Job to run via a Job Button, only the model-level `extras.run_job` permission is checked (i.e., does the user have permission to run Jobs in general?). Object-level permissions (i.e., does the user have permission to run this *specific* Job?) are not enforced by the URL/view used in this case (`/extras/job-button/<uuid>/run/`) The effect is that a user with permissions to run even a single Job can actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\n> Not all Jobs can be configured as JobButtons; only those implemented as subclasses of `JobButtonReceiver` can be used in this way, so this vulnerability only applies specifically to `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses.\n\nAdditionally, although the documentation states that both `extras.run_job` permission and `extras.run_jobbutton` permission must be granted to a user in order to run Jobs via JobButton, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is not actually enforced by the view code, only by the UI by disabling the button from being clicked normally. Furthermore, the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission never prevented invoking Jobs (including `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses) via the normal \"Job Run\" UI, so after some discussion, we've decided that the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission is redundant, and as it never achieved its stated/documented purpose, the fixes below will remove the UI check for `extras.run_jobbutton` and all other references to the `extras.run_jobbutton` permission, rather than adding enforcement of this previously unenforced permission.\n\n### Patches\n_Has the problem been patched? What versions should users upgrade to?_\n\nFix will be available in Nautobot 1.6.8 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995) and 2.1.0 (https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993)\n\n### Workarounds\n_Is there a way for users to fix or remediate the vulnerability without upgrading?_\n\nPartial mitigation can be achieved by auditing `JobButtonReceiver` subclasses defined in the system and restricting which users are permitted to create or edit JobButton records. \n\n### References\n\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/issues/4988\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4993\n- https://github.com/nautobot/nautobot/pull/4995\n", "file_change_id": 272243535840167, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vf5m-xrhm-v999", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "get_required_permission", "code_before": "    def get_required_permission(self):\n        return \"extras.run_job\"\n", "code_after": ""}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the vulnerability in question pertains to a lack of object-level permissions enforcement when running a Job via a Job Button in the Nautobot system. Specifically, a user with permissions to run even a single Job could actually run all configured JobButton Jobs.\n\nThe function `post` is removed in the commit, which was responsible for enqueuing a job and redirecting the user. This function was directly involved in the process of running a job, and it did not include any checks for object-level permissions. Therefore, it can be inferred that this function was vulnerable.\n\nThe commit message and the description of the vulnerability both support this conclusion. The commit message mentions a fix for object permissions enforcement on Job Buttons, and the vulnerability description details the lack of object-level permissions enforcement.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1, indicating that the function is vulnerable.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "verify_mode", "code_before": "    def verify_mode(self):\n        return self._ctx.get_verify_mode()\n", "code_after": "    def verify_mode(self):\n        return _openssl_to_stdlib_verify[self._ctx.get_verify_mode()]\n", "commit_message": "CVE-2016-9015: Correct set verify flags.\n\nThis error, introduced in 1.17, causes users using OpenSSL 1.1.0 along\nwith PyOpenSSL to silently fail to validate certificates. This is an\nalarming failure mode, and is currently being worked on as part of\nOpenSSL 1.1.0: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/1793\n\nThis patch will be merged into master in a different form, as part of a\ngeneral testing cleanup. The flaw was discovered by Cory Benfield, and\nwas in fact caught by urllib3's test suite: it just happens that we\nhadn't tested that particular software configuration before releasing\n1.17.", "commit": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/commit/c32cdbc16a9634fa0f8c829d1270301570158715", "description": "Versions 1.17 and 1.18 of the Python urllib3 library suffer from a vulnerability that can cause them, in certain configurations, to not correctly validate TLS certificates. This places users of the library with those configurations at risk of man-in-the-middle and information leakage attacks. This vulnerability affects users using versions 1.17 and 1.18 of the urllib3 library, who are using the optional PyOpenSSL support for TLS instead of the regular standard library TLS backend, and who are using OpenSSL 1.1.0 via PyOpenSSL. This is an extremely uncommon configuration, so the security impact of this vulnerability is low.", "file_change_id": 41252458108365, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v4w5-p2hg-8fh6", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `verify_mode` has been modified. The change involves the return value of the function, which now maps the OpenSSL verification mode to the standard library verification mode.\n\nThe commit message and vulnerability description suggest that the issue was with the incorrect validation of TLS certificates when using OpenSSL 1.1.0 with PyOpenSSL. This could potentially lead to man-in-the-middle and information leakage attacks.\n\nGiven that the `verify_mode` function is directly related to the verification mode, and the change in the function is about the verification mode mapping, it can be inferred that this function was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1, indicating that the function is vulnerable.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "verify_mode", "code_before": "    def verify_mode(self, value):\n        self._ctx.set_verify(value, _verify_callback)\n", "code_after": "    def verify_mode(self, value):\n        self._ctx.set_verify(\n            _stdlib_to_openssl_verify[value], _verify_callback\n        )\n", "commit_message": "CVE-2016-9015: Correct set verify flags.\n\nThis error, introduced in 1.17, causes users using OpenSSL 1.1.0 along\nwith PyOpenSSL to silently fail to validate certificates. This is an\nalarming failure mode, and is currently being worked on as part of\nOpenSSL 1.1.0: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/1793\n\nThis patch will be merged into master in a different form, as part of a\ngeneral testing cleanup. The flaw was discovered by Cory Benfield, and\nwas in fact caught by urllib3's test suite: it just happens that we\nhadn't tested that particular software configuration before releasing\n1.17.", "commit": "https://github.com/urllib3/urllib3/commit/c32cdbc16a9634fa0f8c829d1270301570158715", "description": "Versions 1.17 and 1.18 of the Python urllib3 library suffer from a vulnerability that can cause them, in certain configurations, to not correctly validate TLS certificates. This places users of the library with those configurations at risk of man-in-the-middle and information leakage attacks. This vulnerability affects users using versions 1.17 and 1.18 of the urllib3 library, who are using the optional PyOpenSSL support for TLS instead of the regular standard library TLS backend, and who are using OpenSSL 1.1.0 via PyOpenSSL. This is an extremely uncommon configuration, so the security impact of this vulnerability is low.", "file_change_id": 41252458108365, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v4w5-p2hg-8fh6", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function \"verify_mode\" was changed in response to a vulnerability (CVE-2016-9015) that caused the system to fail to validate certificates silently when using OpenSSL 1.1.0 along with PyOpenSSL. This vulnerability could potentially expose users to man-in-the-middle and information leakage attacks.\n\nThe change in the function involves the way the verification mode is set. In the \"code_before\", the value is directly passed to the \"set_verify\" method. In the \"code_after\", the value is mapped to an OpenSSL verification mode before being passed to the \"set_verify\" method. This change appears to be directly related to the described vulnerability, as it alters the way certificate verification is handled.\n\nTherefore, it can be concluded that the function \"verify_mode\" was vulnerable and has been changed to address the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "clone", "code_before": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"DictionaryObject\":\n        \"\"\"Clone object into pdf_dest.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"DictionaryObject\",\n            self._reference_clone(self.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        if len(d__.keys()) == 0:\n            d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n        return d__\n", "code_after": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"DictionaryObject\":\n        \"\"\"Clone object into pdf_dest.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"DictionaryObject\",\n            self._reference_clone(self.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        if len(d__.keys()) == 0:\n            d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n        return d__\n", "commit_message": "SEC: Infinite recursion when using PdfWriter(clone_from=reader) (#2264)\n\nUse a visited memo to check if the current object in the clone operation has already been visited, and if so, do not add it to the list of objects.\r\n\r\nThis avoids infinite recursion in case there are links to the same object inside a PDF.", "commit": "https://github.com/py-pdf/pypdf/commit/9b23ac3c9619492570011d551d521690de9a3e2d", "description": "### Impact\nAn attacker who uses this vulnerability can craft a PDF which leads to an infinite loop.\nThis infinite loop blocks the current process and can utilize a single core of the CPU by 100%. It does not affect memory usage.\n\nThat is, for example, the case when the pypdf-user manipulates an incoming malicious PDF e.g. by merging it with another PDF or by adding annotations.\n\n### Patches\nThe issue was fixed with #2264\n\n### Workarounds\nIf you cannot update your version of pypdf, you should modify `pypdf/generic/_data_structures.py` just like #2264 did.", "file_change_id": 171781104309520, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wjcc-cq79-p63f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "_clone", "code_before": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: \"DictionaryObject\",\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src: \"DictionaryObject\":\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        # first we remove for the ignore_fields\n        # that are for a limited number of levels\n        x = 0\n        assert ignore_fields is not None\n        ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n        while x < len(ignore_fields):\n            if isinstance(ignore_fields[x], int):\n                if cast(int, ignore_fields[x]) <= 0:\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    ignore_fields[x] -= 1  # type:ignore\n            x += 1\n        #  First check if this is a chain list, we need to loop to prevent recur\n        if any(\n            field not in ignore_fields\n            and field in src\n            and isinstance(src.raw_get(field), IndirectObject)\n            and isinstance(src[field], DictionaryObject)\n            and (\n                src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                == cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n            )\n            for field in [\"/Next\", \"/Prev\", \"/N\", \"/V\"]\n        ):\n            ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n            for lst in ((\"/Next\", \"/Prev\"), (\"/N\", \"/V\")):\n                for k in lst:\n                    objs = []\n                    if (\n                        k in src\n                        and k not in self\n                        and isinstance(src.raw_get(k), IndirectObject)\n                        and isinstance(src[k], DictionaryObject)\n                        # IF need to go further the idea is to check\n                        # that the types are the same:\n                        and (\n                            src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                            == cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n                        )\n                    ):\n                        cur_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = cast(\n                            \"DictionaryObject\", src[k]\n                        )\n                        prev_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = self\n                        while cur_obj is not None:\n                            clon = cast(\n                                \"DictionaryObject\",\n                                cur_obj._reference_clone(\n                                    cur_obj.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n                                ),\n                            )\n                            objs.append((cur_obj, clon))\n                            assert prev_obj is not None\n                            prev_obj[NameObject(k)] = clon.indirect_reference\n                            prev_obj = clon\n                            try:\n                                if cur_obj == src:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                else:\n                                    cur_obj = cast(\"DictionaryObject\", cur_obj[k])\n                            except Exception:\n                                cur_obj = None\n                        for s, c in objs:\n                            c._clone(s, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n\n        for k, v in src.items():\n            if k not in ignore_fields:\n                if isinstance(v, StreamObject):\n                    if not hasattr(v, \"indirect_reference\"):\n                        v.indirect_reference = None\n                    vv = v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                    assert vv.indirect_reference is not None\n                    self[k.clone(pdf_dest)] = vv.indirect_reference  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n                elif k not in self:\n                    self[NameObject(k)] = (\n                        v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                        if hasattr(v, \"clone\")\n                        else v\n                    )\n", "code_after": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: \"DictionaryObject\",\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src: \"DictionaryObject\":\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        # first we remove for the ignore_fields\n        # that are for a limited number of levels\n        x = 0\n        assert ignore_fields is not None\n        ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n        while x < len(ignore_fields):\n            if isinstance(ignore_fields[x], int):\n                if cast(int, ignore_fields[x]) <= 0:\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    ignore_fields[x] -= 1  # type:ignore\n            x += 1\n        #  First check if this is a chain list, we need to loop to prevent recur\n        if any(\n            field not in ignore_fields\n            and field in src\n            and isinstance(src.raw_get(field), IndirectObject)\n            and isinstance(src[field], DictionaryObject)\n            and (\n                src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                == cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n            )\n            for field in [\"/Next\", \"/Prev\", \"/N\", \"/V\"]\n        ):\n            ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n            for lst in ((\"/Next\", \"/Prev\"), (\"/N\", \"/V\")):\n                for k in lst:\n                    objs = []\n                    if (\n                        k in src\n                        and k not in self\n                        and isinstance(src.raw_get(k), IndirectObject)\n                        and isinstance(src[k], DictionaryObject)\n                        # IF need to go further the idea is to check\n                        # that the types are the same:\n                        and (\n                            src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                            == cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n                        )\n                    ):\n                        cur_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = cast(\n                            \"DictionaryObject\", src[k]\n                        )\n                        prev_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = self\n                        while cur_obj is not None:\n                            clon = cast(\n                                \"DictionaryObject\",\n                                cur_obj._reference_clone(\n                                    cur_obj.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n                                ),\n                            )\n                            # check to see if we've previously processed our item\n                            if clon.indirect_reference is not None:\n                                idnum = clon.indirect_reference.idnum\n                                generation = clon.indirect_reference.generation\n                                if (idnum, generation) in visited:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                    break\n                                visited.add((idnum, generation))\n                            objs.append((cur_obj, clon))\n                            assert prev_obj is not None\n                            prev_obj[NameObject(k)] = clon.indirect_reference\n                            prev_obj = clon\n                            try:\n                                if cur_obj == src:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                else:\n                                    cur_obj = cast(\"DictionaryObject\", cur_obj[k])\n                            except Exception:\n                                cur_obj = None\n                        for s, c in objs:\n                            c._clone(s, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n\n        for k, v in src.items():\n            if k not in ignore_fields:\n                if isinstance(v, StreamObject):\n                    if not hasattr(v, \"indirect_reference\"):\n                        v.indirect_reference = None\n                    vv = v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                    assert vv.indirect_reference is not None\n                    self[k.clone(pdf_dest)] = vv.indirect_reference  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n                elif k not in self:\n                    self[NameObject(k)] = (\n                        v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                        if hasattr(v, \"clone\")\n                        else v\n                    )\n"}, {"function_name": "_clone", "code_before": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: DictionaryObject,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        self._data = cast(\"StreamObject\", src)._data\n        try:\n            decoded_self = cast(\"StreamObject\", src).decoded_self\n            if decoded_self is None:\n                self.decoded_self = None\n            else:\n                self.decoded_self = cast(\n                    \"DecodedStreamObject\",\n                    decoded_self.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields),\n                )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        super()._clone(src, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n", "code_after": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: DictionaryObject,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        self._data = cast(\"StreamObject\", src)._data\n        try:\n            decoded_self = cast(\"StreamObject\", src).decoded_self\n            if decoded_self is None:\n                self.decoded_self = None\n            else:\n                self.decoded_self = cast(\n                    \"DecodedStreamObject\",\n                    decoded_self.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields),\n                )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        super()._clone(src, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described in the advisory is related to an infinite loop that can be triggered by a maliciously crafted PDF, leading to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack by utilizing 100% of a CPU core.\n\nThe changes in the \"clone\" function and the other two \"_clone\" functions in the commit are directly related to this vulnerability. The changes introduce a \"visited\" set that keeps track of the objects that have been processed during the clone operation. This set is used to prevent revisiting the same object, which would cause an infinite loop.\n\nIn the \"clone\" function, a \"visited\" set is initialized and passed to the \"_clone\" function. In the \"_clone\" functions, the \"visited\" set is checked before processing an object, and if the object has already been processed (i.e., it is in the \"visited\" set), it is skipped, thus preventing an infinite loop.\n\nTherefore, the \"clone\" function and the other two \"_clone\" functions are vulnerable and have been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_clone", "code_before": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: \"DictionaryObject\",\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src: \"DictionaryObject\":\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        # first we remove for the ignore_fields\n        # that are for a limited number of levels\n        x = 0\n        assert ignore_fields is not None\n        ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n        while x < len(ignore_fields):\n            if isinstance(ignore_fields[x], int):\n                if cast(int, ignore_fields[x]) <= 0:\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    ignore_fields[x] -= 1  # type:ignore\n            x += 1\n        #  First check if this is a chain list, we need to loop to prevent recur\n        if any(\n            field not in ignore_fields\n            and field in src\n            and isinstance(src.raw_get(field), IndirectObject)\n            and isinstance(src[field], DictionaryObject)\n            and (\n                src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                == cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n            )\n            for field in [\"/Next\", \"/Prev\", \"/N\", \"/V\"]\n        ):\n            ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n            for lst in ((\"/Next\", \"/Prev\"), (\"/N\", \"/V\")):\n                for k in lst:\n                    objs = []\n                    if (\n                        k in src\n                        and k not in self\n                        and isinstance(src.raw_get(k), IndirectObject)\n                        and isinstance(src[k], DictionaryObject)\n                        # IF need to go further the idea is to check\n                        # that the types are the same:\n                        and (\n                            src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                            == cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n                        )\n                    ):\n                        cur_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = cast(\n                            \"DictionaryObject\", src[k]\n                        )\n                        prev_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = self\n                        while cur_obj is not None:\n                            clon = cast(\n                                \"DictionaryObject\",\n                                cur_obj._reference_clone(\n                                    cur_obj.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n                                ),\n                            )\n                            objs.append((cur_obj, clon))\n                            assert prev_obj is not None\n                            prev_obj[NameObject(k)] = clon.indirect_reference\n                            prev_obj = clon\n                            try:\n                                if cur_obj == src:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                else:\n                                    cur_obj = cast(\"DictionaryObject\", cur_obj[k])\n                            except Exception:\n                                cur_obj = None\n                        for s, c in objs:\n                            c._clone(s, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n\n        for k, v in src.items():\n            if k not in ignore_fields:\n                if isinstance(v, StreamObject):\n                    if not hasattr(v, \"indirect_reference\"):\n                        v.indirect_reference = None\n                    vv = v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                    assert vv.indirect_reference is not None\n                    self[k.clone(pdf_dest)] = vv.indirect_reference  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n                elif k not in self:\n                    self[NameObject(k)] = (\n                        v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                        if hasattr(v, \"clone\")\n                        else v\n                    )\n", "code_after": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: \"DictionaryObject\",\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src: \"DictionaryObject\":\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        # first we remove for the ignore_fields\n        # that are for a limited number of levels\n        x = 0\n        assert ignore_fields is not None\n        ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n        while x < len(ignore_fields):\n            if isinstance(ignore_fields[x], int):\n                if cast(int, ignore_fields[x]) <= 0:\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    ignore_fields[x] -= 1  # type:ignore\n            x += 1\n        #  First check if this is a chain list, we need to loop to prevent recur\n        if any(\n            field not in ignore_fields\n            and field in src\n            and isinstance(src.raw_get(field), IndirectObject)\n            and isinstance(src[field], DictionaryObject)\n            and (\n                src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                == cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n            )\n            for field in [\"/Next\", \"/Prev\", \"/N\", \"/V\"]\n        ):\n            ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n            for lst in ((\"/Next\", \"/Prev\"), (\"/N\", \"/V\")):\n                for k in lst:\n                    objs = []\n                    if (\n                        k in src\n                        and k not in self\n                        and isinstance(src.raw_get(k), IndirectObject)\n                        and isinstance(src[k], DictionaryObject)\n                        # IF need to go further the idea is to check\n                        # that the types are the same:\n                        and (\n                            src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                            == cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n                        )\n                    ):\n                        cur_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = cast(\n                            \"DictionaryObject\", src[k]\n                        )\n                        prev_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = self\n                        while cur_obj is not None:\n                            clon = cast(\n                                \"DictionaryObject\",\n                                cur_obj._reference_clone(\n                                    cur_obj.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n                                ),\n                            )\n                            # check to see if we've previously processed our item\n                            if clon.indirect_reference is not None:\n                                idnum = clon.indirect_reference.idnum\n                                generation = clon.indirect_reference.generation\n                                if (idnum, generation) in visited:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                    break\n                                visited.add((idnum, generation))\n                            objs.append((cur_obj, clon))\n                            assert prev_obj is not None\n                            prev_obj[NameObject(k)] = clon.indirect_reference\n                            prev_obj = clon\n                            try:\n                                if cur_obj == src:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                else:\n                                    cur_obj = cast(\"DictionaryObject\", cur_obj[k])\n                            except Exception:\n                                cur_obj = None\n                        for s, c in objs:\n                            c._clone(s, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n\n        for k, v in src.items():\n            if k not in ignore_fields:\n                if isinstance(v, StreamObject):\n                    if not hasattr(v, \"indirect_reference\"):\n                        v.indirect_reference = None\n                    vv = v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                    assert vv.indirect_reference is not None\n                    self[k.clone(pdf_dest)] = vv.indirect_reference  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n                elif k not in self:\n                    self[NameObject(k)] = (\n                        v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                        if hasattr(v, \"clone\")\n                        else v\n                    )\n", "commit_message": "SEC: Infinite recursion when using PdfWriter(clone_from=reader) (#2264)\n\nUse a visited memo to check if the current object in the clone operation has already been visited, and if so, do not add it to the list of objects.\r\n\r\nThis avoids infinite recursion in case there are links to the same object inside a PDF.", "commit": "https://github.com/py-pdf/pypdf/commit/9b23ac3c9619492570011d551d521690de9a3e2d", "description": "### Impact\nAn attacker who uses this vulnerability can craft a PDF which leads to an infinite loop.\nThis infinite loop blocks the current process and can utilize a single core of the CPU by 100%. It does not affect memory usage.\n\nThat is, for example, the case when the pypdf-user manipulates an incoming malicious PDF e.g. by merging it with another PDF or by adding annotations.\n\n### Patches\nThe issue was fixed with #2264\n\n### Workarounds\nIf you cannot update your version of pypdf, you should modify `pypdf/generic/_data_structures.py` just like #2264 did.", "file_change_id": 171781104309520, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wjcc-cq79-p63f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "clone", "code_before": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"DictionaryObject\":\n        \"\"\"Clone object into pdf_dest.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"DictionaryObject\",\n            self._reference_clone(self.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        if len(d__.keys()) == 0:\n            d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n        return d__\n", "code_after": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"DictionaryObject\":\n        \"\"\"Clone object into pdf_dest.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"DictionaryObject\",\n            self._reference_clone(self.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        if len(d__.keys()) == 0:\n            d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n        return d__\n"}, {"function_name": "clone", "code_before": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: Any,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"ContentStream\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Clone object into pdf_dest.\n\n        Args:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n\n        Returns:\n            The cloned ContentStream\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"ContentStream\",\n            self._reference_clone(\n                self.__class__(None, None), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n            ),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n        return d__\n", "code_after": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: Any,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"ContentStream\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Clone object into pdf_dest.\n\n        Args:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n\n        Returns:\n            The cloned ContentStream\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"ContentStream\",\n            self._reference_clone(\n                self.__class__(None, None), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n            ),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n        return d__\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The function \"_clone\" is indeed relevant to the vulnerability that the commit is trying to fix. The vulnerability is about an infinite loop that can be triggered by a maliciously crafted PDF, which can lead to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack by utilizing a single core of the CPU by 100%.\n\nThe changes in the \"_clone\" function are directly addressing this issue. A new parameter \"visited\" is added to the function, which is a set used to keep track of the objects that have already been processed. This is a common technique to prevent infinite recursion when dealing with data structures that may contain cycles, which is exactly the issue here.\n\nIn the new version of the function, before an object is processed, it is checked whether it has already been visited. If it has, the function immediately returns, effectively breaking the cycle and preventing the infinite loop.\n\nTherefore, the function \"_clone\" is vulnerable and the changes are relevant to the vulnerability that the commit is trying to fix.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "clone", "code_before": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: Any,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"ContentStream\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Clone object into pdf_dest.\n\n        Args:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n\n        Returns:\n            The cloned ContentStream\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"ContentStream\",\n            self._reference_clone(\n                self.__class__(None, None), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n            ),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n        return d__\n", "code_after": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: Any,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"ContentStream\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Clone object into pdf_dest.\n\n        Args:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n\n        Returns:\n            The cloned ContentStream\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"ContentStream\",\n            self._reference_clone(\n                self.__class__(None, None), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n            ),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n        return d__\n", "commit_message": "SEC: Infinite recursion when using PdfWriter(clone_from=reader) (#2264)\n\nUse a visited memo to check if the current object in the clone operation has already been visited, and if so, do not add it to the list of objects.\r\n\r\nThis avoids infinite recursion in case there are links to the same object inside a PDF.", "commit": "https://github.com/py-pdf/pypdf/commit/9b23ac3c9619492570011d551d521690de9a3e2d", "description": "### Impact\nAn attacker who uses this vulnerability can craft a PDF which leads to an infinite loop.\nThis infinite loop blocks the current process and can utilize a single core of the CPU by 100%. It does not affect memory usage.\n\nThat is, for example, the case when the pypdf-user manipulates an incoming malicious PDF e.g. by merging it with another PDF or by adding annotations.\n\n### Patches\nThe issue was fixed with #2264\n\n### Workarounds\nIf you cannot update your version of pypdf, you should modify `pypdf/generic/_data_structures.py` just like #2264 did.", "file_change_id": 171781104309520, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wjcc-cq79-p63f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "_clone", "code_before": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: \"DictionaryObject\",\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src: \"DictionaryObject\":\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        # first we remove for the ignore_fields\n        # that are for a limited number of levels\n        x = 0\n        assert ignore_fields is not None\n        ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n        while x < len(ignore_fields):\n            if isinstance(ignore_fields[x], int):\n                if cast(int, ignore_fields[x]) <= 0:\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    ignore_fields[x] -= 1  # type:ignore\n            x += 1\n        #  First check if this is a chain list, we need to loop to prevent recur\n        if any(\n            field not in ignore_fields\n            and field in src\n            and isinstance(src.raw_get(field), IndirectObject)\n            and isinstance(src[field], DictionaryObject)\n            and (\n                src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                == cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n            )\n            for field in [\"/Next\", \"/Prev\", \"/N\", \"/V\"]\n        ):\n            ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n            for lst in ((\"/Next\", \"/Prev\"), (\"/N\", \"/V\")):\n                for k in lst:\n                    objs = []\n                    if (\n                        k in src\n                        and k not in self\n                        and isinstance(src.raw_get(k), IndirectObject)\n                        and isinstance(src[k], DictionaryObject)\n                        # IF need to go further the idea is to check\n                        # that the types are the same:\n                        and (\n                            src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                            == cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n                        )\n                    ):\n                        cur_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = cast(\n                            \"DictionaryObject\", src[k]\n                        )\n                        prev_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = self\n                        while cur_obj is not None:\n                            clon = cast(\n                                \"DictionaryObject\",\n                                cur_obj._reference_clone(\n                                    cur_obj.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n                                ),\n                            )\n                            objs.append((cur_obj, clon))\n                            assert prev_obj is not None\n                            prev_obj[NameObject(k)] = clon.indirect_reference\n                            prev_obj = clon\n                            try:\n                                if cur_obj == src:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                else:\n                                    cur_obj = cast(\"DictionaryObject\", cur_obj[k])\n                            except Exception:\n                                cur_obj = None\n                        for s, c in objs:\n                            c._clone(s, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n\n        for k, v in src.items():\n            if k not in ignore_fields:\n                if isinstance(v, StreamObject):\n                    if not hasattr(v, \"indirect_reference\"):\n                        v.indirect_reference = None\n                    vv = v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                    assert vv.indirect_reference is not None\n                    self[k.clone(pdf_dest)] = vv.indirect_reference  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n                elif k not in self:\n                    self[NameObject(k)] = (\n                        v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                        if hasattr(v, \"clone\")\n                        else v\n                    )\n", "code_after": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: \"DictionaryObject\",\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src: \"DictionaryObject\":\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        # first we remove for the ignore_fields\n        # that are for a limited number of levels\n        x = 0\n        assert ignore_fields is not None\n        ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n        while x < len(ignore_fields):\n            if isinstance(ignore_fields[x], int):\n                if cast(int, ignore_fields[x]) <= 0:\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    del ignore_fields[x]\n                    continue\n                else:\n                    ignore_fields[x] -= 1  # type:ignore\n            x += 1\n        #  First check if this is a chain list, we need to loop to prevent recur\n        if any(\n            field not in ignore_fields\n            and field in src\n            and isinstance(src.raw_get(field), IndirectObject)\n            and isinstance(src[field], DictionaryObject)\n            and (\n                src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                == cast(DictionaryObject, src[field]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n            )\n            for field in [\"/Next\", \"/Prev\", \"/N\", \"/V\"]\n        ):\n            ignore_fields = list(ignore_fields)\n            for lst in ((\"/Next\", \"/Prev\"), (\"/N\", \"/V\")):\n                for k in lst:\n                    objs = []\n                    if (\n                        k in src\n                        and k not in self\n                        and isinstance(src.raw_get(k), IndirectObject)\n                        and isinstance(src[k], DictionaryObject)\n                        # IF need to go further the idea is to check\n                        # that the types are the same:\n                        and (\n                            src.get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None) is None\n                            or src.get(\"/Type\", None)\n                            == cast(DictionaryObject, src[k]).get(\"/Type\", None)\n                        )\n                    ):\n                        cur_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = cast(\n                            \"DictionaryObject\", src[k]\n                        )\n                        prev_obj: Optional[DictionaryObject] = self\n                        while cur_obj is not None:\n                            clon = cast(\n                                \"DictionaryObject\",\n                                cur_obj._reference_clone(\n                                    cur_obj.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n                                ),\n                            )\n                            # check to see if we've previously processed our item\n                            if clon.indirect_reference is not None:\n                                idnum = clon.indirect_reference.idnum\n                                generation = clon.indirect_reference.generation\n                                if (idnum, generation) in visited:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                    break\n                                visited.add((idnum, generation))\n                            objs.append((cur_obj, clon))\n                            assert prev_obj is not None\n                            prev_obj[NameObject(k)] = clon.indirect_reference\n                            prev_obj = clon\n                            try:\n                                if cur_obj == src:\n                                    cur_obj = None\n                                else:\n                                    cur_obj = cast(\"DictionaryObject\", cur_obj[k])\n                            except Exception:\n                                cur_obj = None\n                        for s, c in objs:\n                            c._clone(s, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n\n        for k, v in src.items():\n            if k not in ignore_fields:\n                if isinstance(v, StreamObject):\n                    if not hasattr(v, \"indirect_reference\"):\n                        v.indirect_reference = None\n                    vv = v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                    assert vv.indirect_reference is not None\n                    self[k.clone(pdf_dest)] = vv.indirect_reference  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n                elif k not in self:\n                    self[NameObject(k)] = (\n                        v.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n                        if hasattr(v, \"clone\")\n                        else v\n                    )\n"}, {"function_name": "_clone", "code_before": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: DictionaryObject,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        self._data = cast(\"StreamObject\", src)._data\n        try:\n            decoded_self = cast(\"StreamObject\", src).decoded_self\n            if decoded_self is None:\n                self.decoded_self = None\n            else:\n                self.decoded_self = cast(\n                    \"DecodedStreamObject\",\n                    decoded_self.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields),\n                )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        super()._clone(src, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n", "code_after": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: DictionaryObject,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        self._data = cast(\"StreamObject\", src)._data\n        try:\n            decoded_self = cast(\"StreamObject\", src).decoded_self\n            if decoded_self is None:\n                self.decoded_self = None\n            else:\n                self.decoded_self = cast(\n                    \"DecodedStreamObject\",\n                    decoded_self.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields),\n                )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        super()._clone(src, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described in the advisory is related to an infinite loop that can be triggered when manipulating a malicious PDF, for example, by merging it with another PDF or adding annotations. This infinite loop can block the current process and utilize a single core of the CPU by 100%.\n\nThe changes in the \"clone\" function are related to the introduction of a \"visited\" set that is used to track whether an object has already been visited during the clone operation. If an object has already been visited, it is not added to the list of objects to be cloned. This change is designed to prevent the infinite loop that was previously possible.\n\nThe other functions changed in the commit (\"_clone\") also include the addition of the \"visited\" parameter and its use in the function logic, which is consistent with the changes in the \"clone\" function.\n\nGiven this information, it can be concluded that the \"clone\" function was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_clone", "code_before": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: DictionaryObject,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        self._data = cast(\"StreamObject\", src)._data\n        try:\n            decoded_self = cast(\"StreamObject\", src).decoded_self\n            if decoded_self is None:\n                self.decoded_self = None\n            else:\n                self.decoded_self = cast(\n                    \"DecodedStreamObject\",\n                    decoded_self.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields),\n                )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        super()._clone(src, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n", "code_after": "    def _clone(\n        self,\n        src: DictionaryObject,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]],\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the object from src.\n\n        Args:\n            src:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n        \"\"\"\n        self._data = cast(\"StreamObject\", src)._data\n        try:\n            decoded_self = cast(\"StreamObject\", src).decoded_self\n            if decoded_self is None:\n                self.decoded_self = None\n            else:\n                self.decoded_self = cast(\n                    \"DecodedStreamObject\",\n                    decoded_self.clone(pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields),\n                )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        super()._clone(src, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n", "commit_message": "SEC: Infinite recursion when using PdfWriter(clone_from=reader) (#2264)\n\nUse a visited memo to check if the current object in the clone operation has already been visited, and if so, do not add it to the list of objects.\r\n\r\nThis avoids infinite recursion in case there are links to the same object inside a PDF.", "commit": "https://github.com/py-pdf/pypdf/commit/9b23ac3c9619492570011d551d521690de9a3e2d", "description": "### Impact\nAn attacker who uses this vulnerability can craft a PDF which leads to an infinite loop.\nThis infinite loop blocks the current process and can utilize a single core of the CPU by 100%. It does not affect memory usage.\n\nThat is, for example, the case when the pypdf-user manipulates an incoming malicious PDF e.g. by merging it with another PDF or by adding annotations.\n\n### Patches\nThe issue was fixed with #2264\n\n### Workarounds\nIf you cannot update your version of pypdf, you should modify `pypdf/generic/_data_structures.py` just like #2264 did.", "file_change_id": 171781104309520, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wjcc-cq79-p63f", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "clone", "code_before": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"DictionaryObject\":\n        \"\"\"Clone object into pdf_dest.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"DictionaryObject\",\n            self._reference_clone(self.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        if len(d__.keys()) == 0:\n            d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n        return d__\n", "code_after": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: PdfWriterProtocol,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"DictionaryObject\":\n        \"\"\"Clone object into pdf_dest.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"DictionaryObject\",\n            self._reference_clone(self.__class__(), pdf_dest, force_duplicate),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        if len(d__.keys()) == 0:\n            d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n        return d__\n"}, {"function_name": "clone", "code_before": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: Any,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"ContentStream\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Clone object into pdf_dest.\n\n        Args:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n\n        Returns:\n            The cloned ContentStream\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"ContentStream\",\n            self._reference_clone(\n                self.__class__(None, None), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n            ),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields)\n        return d__\n", "code_after": "    def clone(\n        self,\n        pdf_dest: Any,\n        force_duplicate: bool = False,\n        ignore_fields: Optional[Sequence[Union[str, int]]] = (),\n    ) -> \"ContentStream\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Clone object into pdf_dest.\n\n        Args:\n            pdf_dest:\n            force_duplicate:\n            ignore_fields:\n\n        Returns:\n            The cloned ContentStream\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            if self.indirect_reference.pdf == pdf_dest and not force_duplicate:  # type: ignore\n                return self\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n        visited: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n        d__ = cast(\n            \"ContentStream\",\n            self._reference_clone(\n                self.__class__(None, None), pdf_dest, force_duplicate\n            ),\n        )\n        if ignore_fields is None:\n            ignore_fields = []\n        d__._clone(self, pdf_dest, force_duplicate, ignore_fields, visited)\n        return d__\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the vulnerability was causing an infinite loop due to the lack of a mechanism to check if an object has already been visited during the clone operation. The commit message and the description of the vulnerability confirm this.\n\nLooking at the changes in the \"_clone\" function, a new parameter \"visited\" has been added. This parameter is a set that keeps track of the objects that have been visited during the clone operation. This change directly addresses the vulnerability by preventing the infinite loop from occurring.\n\nTherefore, the \"_clone\" function was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "authenticate", "code_before": "    def authenticate(self, request, username=None, code=None, **kwargs):\n        if username is None:\n            username = kwargs.get(get_user_model().USERNAME_FIELD)\n\n        if not username or not code:\n            return\n\n        try:\n            user = get_user_model()._default_manager.get_by_natural_key(username)\n\n            if not self.user_can_authenticate(user):\n                return\n\n            timeout = getattr(settings, 'NOPASSWORD_LOGIN_CODE_TIMEOUT', 900)\n            timestamp = timezone.now() - timedelta(seconds=timeout)\n\n            # We don't delete the login code when authenticating,\n            # as that is done during validation of the login form\n            # and validation should not have any side effects.\n            # It is the responsibility of the view/form to delete the token\n            # as soon as the login was successfull.\n            user.login_code = LoginCode.objects.get(user=user, code=code, timestamp__gt=timestamp)\n\n            return user\n\n        except (get_user_model().DoesNotExist, LoginCode.DoesNotExist):\n            return\n", "code_after": "    def authenticate(self, request, username=None, code=None, **kwargs):\n        if username is None:\n            username = kwargs.get(get_user_model().USERNAME_FIELD)\n\n        if not username or not code:\n            return\n\n        try:\n            user = get_user_model()._default_manager.get_by_natural_key(username)\n\n            if not self.user_can_authenticate(user):\n                return\n\n            timeout = getattr(settings, 'NOPASSWORD_LOGIN_CODE_TIMEOUT', 900)\n            timestamp = timezone.now() - timedelta(seconds=timeout)\n\n            # We don't delete the login code when authenticating,\n            # as that is done during validation of the login form\n            # and validation should not have any side effects.\n            # It is the responsibility of the view/form to delete the token\n            # as soon as the login was successful.\n\n            for c in LoginCode.objects.filter(user=user, timestamp__gt=timestamp):\n                if c.code == code:\n                    user.login_code = c\n                    return user\n            return\n\n        except (get_user_model().DoesNotExist, LoginCode.DoesNotExist):\n            return\n", "commit_message": "fix: Remove code from database", "commit": "https://github.com/relekang/django-nopassword/commit/d8b4615f5fbfe3997d96cf4cb3e342406396193c", "description": "django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.", "file_change_id": 130010429891130, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-37cf-r3w2-gjfw", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the \"authenticate\" function has been modified to address a vulnerability. The vulnerability description states that \"django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.\" \n\nIn the \"code_before\" snippet, the function retrieves a LoginCode object directly using the provided code. This implies that the code was stored in the database in cleartext, which aligns with the vulnerability description.\n\nIn the \"code_after\" snippet, the function has been modified to iterate over LoginCode objects associated with the user and timestamp, and only when the provided code matches one of these objects, it is assigned to the user's login_code. This change suggests that the codes are no longer directly retrievable from the database using the provided code, which would be a way to address the described vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, it can be concluded that the \"authenticate\" function was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "clean_code", "code_before": "    def clean_code(self):\n        code = self.cleaned_data['code']\n        username = code.user.get_username()\n        user = authenticate(self.request, **{\n            get_user_model().USERNAME_FIELD: username,\n            'code': code.code,\n        })\n\n        if not user:\n            raise forms.ValidationError(\n                self.error_messages['invalid_code'],\n                code='invalid_code',\n            )\n\n        self.cleaned_data['user'] = user\n\n        return code\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "fix: Remove code from database", "commit": "https://github.com/relekang/django-nopassword/commit/d8b4615f5fbfe3997d96cf4cb3e342406396193c", "description": "django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.", "file_change_id": 161834012175040, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-37cf-r3w2-gjfw", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "save", "code_before": "    def save(self):\n        self.cleaned_data['code'].delete()\n", "code_after": "    def save(self):\n        if self.get_user().login_code:\n            self.get_user().login_code.delete()\n"}, {"function_name": "save", "code_before": "    def save(self, request, login_code_url='login_code', domain_override=None, extra_context=None):\n        login_code = models.LoginCode.create_code_for_user(\n            user=self.cleaned_data['user'],\n            next=self.cleaned_data['next'],\n        )\n\n        if not domain_override:\n            current_site = get_current_site(request)\n            site_name = current_site.name\n            domain = current_site.domain\n        else:\n            site_name = domain = domain_override\n\n        url = '{}://{}{}?code={}'.format(\n            'https' if request.is_secure() else 'http',\n            domain,\n            resolve_url(login_code_url),\n            login_code.code,\n        )\n\n        context = {\n            'domain': domain,\n            'site_name': site_name,\n            'code': login_code.code,\n            'url': url,\n        }\n\n        if extra_context:\n            context.update(extra_context)\n\n        self.send_login_code(login_code, context)\n\n        return login_code\n", "code_after": "    def save(self, request, login_code_url='login_code', domain_override=None, extra_context=None):\n        login_code = models.LoginCode.create_code_for_user(\n            user=self.cleaned_data['user'],\n            next=self.cleaned_data['next'],\n        )\n\n        if not domain_override:\n            current_site = get_current_site(request)\n            site_name = current_site.name\n            domain = current_site.domain\n        else:\n            site_name = domain = domain_override\n\n        url = '{}://{}{}?user={}&code={}'.format(\n            'https' if request.is_secure() else 'http',\n            domain,\n            resolve_url(login_code_url),\n            login_code.user.pk,\n            login_code.code,\n        )\n\n        context = {\n            'domain': domain,\n            'site_name': site_name,\n            'code': login_code.code,\n            'url': url,\n        }\n\n        if extra_context:\n            context.update(extra_context)\n\n        self.send_login_code(login_code, context)\n\n        return login_code\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described is that \"django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.\" The commit message indicates that the fix involves removing the code from the database.\n\nLooking at the changes in the commit, the function `clean_code` is completely removed. This function was previously responsible for cleaning the code data and authenticating the user based on the code. The removal of this function is likely related to the vulnerability fix, as it directly involves the handling of the code that was previously stored in the database.\n\nIn addition, other functions in the commit (`save`) are also changed to adjust how the code is handled, further supporting the idea that `clean_code` is relevant to the vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, it can be concluded that the `clean_code` function is vulnerable and has been removed as part of the fix for the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "generate_code", "code_before": "    def generate_code(cls):\n        hash_algorithm = getattr(settings, 'NOPASSWORD_HASH_ALGORITHM', 'sha256')\n        m = getattr(hashlib, hash_algorithm)()\n        m.update(getattr(settings, 'SECRET_KEY', None).encode('utf-8'))\n        m.update(os.urandom(16))\n        if getattr(settings, 'NOPASSWORD_NUMERIC_CODES', False):\n            hashed = str(int(m.hexdigest(), 16))\n        else:\n            hashed = m.hexdigest()\n        return hashed\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "fix: Remove code from database", "commit": "https://github.com/relekang/django-nopassword/commit/d8b4615f5fbfe3997d96cf4cb3e342406396193c", "description": "django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.", "file_change_id": 28912334138907, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-37cf-r3w2-gjfw", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "create_code_for_user", "code_before": "    def create_code_for_user(cls, user, next=None):\n        if not user.is_active:\n            return None\n\n        code = cls.generate_code()\n        login_code = LoginCode(user=user, code=code)\n        if next is not None:\n            login_code.next = next\n        login_code.save()\n        return login_code\n", "code_after": "    def create_code_for_user(cls, user, next=None):\n        if not user.is_active:\n            return None\n\n        login_code = LoginCode(user=user)\n        if next is not None:\n            login_code.next = next\n        login_code.save()\n        return login_code\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described here is that \"django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.\" The commit message \"fix: Remove code from database\" suggests that the commit is intended to address this vulnerability.\n\nLooking at the changes in the functions, the `generate_code` function, which was responsible for generating a code (presumably a secret), has been completely removed. In the `create_code_for_user` function, the line `code = cls.generate_code()` has been removed, and the `code` is no longer being passed to the `LoginCode` constructor. This suggests that the code (or secret) is no longer being generated or stored, which would indeed address the vulnerability of storing cleartext secrets in the database.\n\nTherefore, the `generate_code` function was vulnerable and has been changed to address the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "create_code_for_user", "code_before": "    def create_code_for_user(cls, user, next=None):\n        if not user.is_active:\n            return None\n\n        code = cls.generate_code()\n        login_code = LoginCode(user=user, code=code)\n        if next is not None:\n            login_code.next = next\n        login_code.save()\n        return login_code\n", "code_after": "    def create_code_for_user(cls, user, next=None):\n        if not user.is_active:\n            return None\n\n        login_code = LoginCode(user=user)\n        if next is not None:\n            login_code.next = next\n        login_code.save()\n        return login_code\n", "commit_message": "fix: Remove code from database", "commit": "https://github.com/relekang/django-nopassword/commit/d8b4615f5fbfe3997d96cf4cb3e342406396193c", "description": "django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.", "file_change_id": 28912334138907, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-37cf-r3w2-gjfw", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "generate_code", "code_before": "    def generate_code(cls):\n        hash_algorithm = getattr(settings, 'NOPASSWORD_HASH_ALGORITHM', 'sha256')\n        m = getattr(hashlib, hash_algorithm)()\n        m.update(getattr(settings, 'SECRET_KEY', None).encode('utf-8'))\n        m.update(os.urandom(16))\n        if getattr(settings, 'NOPASSWORD_NUMERIC_CODES', False):\n            hashed = str(int(m.hexdigest(), 16))\n        else:\n            hashed = m.hexdigest()\n        return hashed\n", "code_after": ""}], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `create_code_for_user` has been modified to remove the generation and storage of a code associated with a user. The commit message \"fix: Remove code from database\" and the vulnerability description \"django-nopassword before 5.0.0 stores cleartext secrets in the database.\" suggest that this change is directly related to the vulnerability.\n\nIn the \"before\" version of the function, a code is generated for the user and stored in the database. In the \"after\" version, this code generation and storage is removed. This change directly addresses the vulnerability of storing cleartext secrets in the database.\n\nAdditionally, the function `generate_code` which was responsible for generating the code has been completely removed in the same commit, further supporting the conclusion that these changes are directly addressing the vulnerability.\n\nTherefore, the function `create_code_for_user` is vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "init_routes", "code_before": "    def init_routes(self):\n        # LISTS\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Lists')\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/lists')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'include_bookmarks'],\n                  resp='lists')\n        def get_lists():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            include_bookmarks = request.query.getunicode('include_bookmarks') or 'all'\n\n            lists = collection.get_lists()\n\n            return {\n                'lists': [blist.serialize(include_bookmarks=include_bookmarks)\n                          for blist in lists]\n            }\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/lists')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'include_bookmarks'],\n                  req_desc='List properties',\n                  req=['title', 'desc', 'public', 'before_id'],\n                  resp='list')\n        def add_list():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            blist = collection.create_bookmark_list(request.json)\n\n            collection.mark_updated()\n\n            return {'list': blist.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/list/<list_id>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'include_bookmarks'],\n                  resp='list')\n        def get_list(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_list(blist)\n\n            include_bookmarks = request.query.getunicode('include_bookmarks') or 'all'\n\n            return {'list': blist.serialize(check_slug=list_id,\n                                            include_bookmarks=include_bookmarks)}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='Update List properties',\n                  req=['title', 'desc', 'public'],\n                  resp='list')\n        def update_list(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            blist.update(request.json)\n\n            blist.mark_updated()\n            return {'list': blist.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/list/<list_id>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_list(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            if collection.remove_list(blist):\n                collection.mark_updated()\n                return {'deleted_id': list_id}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'error_deleting')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/move')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['before_id'],\n                  resp='success')\n        def move_list_before(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            before_id = request.json.get('before_id')\n\n            if before_id:\n                before = self.load_list(collection, before_id)\n            else:\n                before = None\n\n            blist.mark_updated()\n            collection.move_list_before(blist, before)\n            return {'success': True}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/lists/reorder')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='An array of existing list ids in a new order',\n                  req=['order'],\n                  resp='success')\n        def reorder_lists():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            new_order = request.json.get('order', [])\n\n            if collection.lists.reorder_objects(new_order):\n                collection.mark_updated()\n                return {'success': True}\n            else:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_order')\n\n\n        #BOOKMARKS\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Bookmarks')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bookmarks')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='Bookmark properties',\n                  req=['title', 'url', 'timestamp', 'browser', 'desc', 'page_id', 'before_id'],\n                  resp='bookmark')\n        def create_bookmark(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            bookmark = blist.create_bookmark(request.json)\n            if bookmark:\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'bookmark': bookmark}\n            else:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_page')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bulk_bookmarks')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='List of Bookmarks',\n                  req={'type': 'array', 'item_type': ['title', 'url', 'timestamp', 'browser', 'desc', 'page_id', 'before_id']},\n                  resp='list')\n        def create_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            bookmark_list = request.json\n\n            blist.add_bookmarks(bookmark_list)\n\n            return {'success': True}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bookmarks')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='bookmarks')\n        def get_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            bookmarks = blist.get_bookmarks()\n\n            return {'bookmarks': bookmarks}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/bookmark/<bid>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'list'],\n                  resp='bookmark')\n        def get_bookmark(bid):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list()\n\n            bookmark = blist.get_bookmark(bid)\n            return {'bookmark': bookmark}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/bookmark/<bid>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'list'],\n                  req_desc='Bookmark properties',\n                  req=['title', 'url', 'timestamp', 'browser', 'desc', 'page_id', 'before_id'],\n                  resp='bookmark')\n        def update_bookmark(bid):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list()\n\n            bookmark = blist.update_bookmark(bid, request.json)\n\n            blist.mark_updated()\n            return {'bookmark': bookmark}\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/bookmark/<bid>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'list'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_bookmark(bid):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list()\n            if blist.remove_bookmark(bid):\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'deleted_id': bid}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(404, 'no_such_bookmark')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bookmarks/reorder')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='An array of existing bookmark ids in a new order',\n                  req=['order'],\n                  resp='success')\n        def reorder_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            new_order = request.json.get('order', [])\n\n            if blist.reorder_bookmarks(new_order):\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'success': True}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_order')\n\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag(None)\n", "code_after": "    def init_routes(self):\n        # LISTS\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Lists')\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/lists')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'include_bookmarks'],\n                  resp='lists')\n        def get_lists():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            include_bookmarks = request.query.getunicode('include_bookmarks') or 'all'\n\n            lists = collection.get_lists()\n\n            return {\n                'lists': [blist.serialize(include_bookmarks=include_bookmarks)\n                          for blist in lists]\n            }\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/lists')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'include_bookmarks'],\n                  req_desc='List properties',\n                  req=['title', 'desc', 'public', 'before_id'],\n                  resp='list')\n        def add_list():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            blist = collection.create_bookmark_list(request.json)\n\n            collection.mark_updated()\n\n            return {'list': blist.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/list/<list_id>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'include_bookmarks'],\n                  resp='list')\n        def get_list(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_list(blist)\n\n            include_bookmarks = request.query.getunicode('include_bookmarks') or 'all'\n\n            return {'list': blist.serialize(check_slug=list_id,\n                                            include_bookmarks=include_bookmarks)}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='Update List properties',\n                  req=['title', 'desc', 'public'],\n                  resp='list')\n        def update_list(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            blist.update(request.json)\n\n            blist.mark_updated()\n            return {'list': blist.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/list/<list_id>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_list(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            if collection.remove_list(blist):\n                collection.mark_updated()\n                return {'deleted_id': list_id}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'error_deleting')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/move')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['before_id'],\n                  resp='success')\n        def move_list_before(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            before_id = request.json.get('before_id')\n\n            if before_id:\n                before = self.load_list(collection, before_id)\n            else:\n                before = None\n\n            blist.mark_updated()\n            collection.move_list_before(blist, before)\n            return {'success': True}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/lists/reorder')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='An array of existing list ids in a new order',\n                  req=['order'],\n                  resp='success')\n        def reorder_lists():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            new_order = request.json.get('order', [])\n\n            if collection.lists.reorder_objects(new_order):\n                collection.mark_updated()\n                return {'success': True}\n            else:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_order')\n\n\n        #BOOKMARKS\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Bookmarks')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bookmarks')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='Bookmark properties',\n                  req=['title', 'url', 'timestamp', 'browser', 'desc', 'page_id', 'before_id'],\n                  resp='bookmark')\n        def create_bookmark(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            bookmark = blist.create_bookmark(request.json)\n            if bookmark:\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'bookmark': bookmark}\n            else:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_page')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bulk_bookmarks')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='List of Bookmarks',\n                  req={'type': 'array', 'item_type': ['title', 'url', 'timestamp', 'browser', 'desc', 'page_id', 'before_id']},\n                  resp='list')\n        def create_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            bookmark_list = request.json\n\n            blist.add_bookmarks(bookmark_list)\n\n            return {'success': True}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bookmarks')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='bookmarks')\n        def get_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            bookmarks = blist.get_bookmarks()\n\n            return {'bookmarks': bookmarks}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/bookmark/<bid>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'list'],\n                  resp='bookmark')\n        def get_bookmark(bid):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list()\n\n            bookmark = blist.get_bookmark(bid)\n            return {'bookmark': bookmark}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/bookmark/<bid>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'list'],\n                  req_desc='Bookmark properties',\n                  req=['title', 'url', 'timestamp', 'browser', 'desc', 'page_id', 'before_id'],\n                  resp='bookmark')\n        def update_bookmark(bid):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list()\n\n            bookmark = blist.update_bookmark(bid, request.json)\n\n            blist.mark_updated()\n            return {'bookmark': bookmark}\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/bookmark/<bid>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll', 'list'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_bookmark(bid):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list()\n            if blist.remove_bookmark(bid):\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'deleted_id': bid}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(404, 'no_such_bookmark')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/list/<list_id>/bookmarks/reorder')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req_desc='An array of existing bookmark ids in a new order',\n                  req=['order'],\n                  resp='success')\n        def reorder_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            new_order = request.json.get('order', [])\n\n            if blist.reorder_bookmarks(new_order):\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'success': True}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_order')\n\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag(None)\n", "commit_message": "Clarify and fix api access", "commit": "https://github.com/rhizome-conifer/conifer/commit/955948b0225c1d7498f83e7da8c592f69d077beb", "description": "Description\nHi there, I would like to report an improper privilege escalation in conifer. Any user can view all recordings of other users.\nProof of Concept\nGo to https://conifer.rhizome.org/ and register 2 accounts, let's call it user1 and user2\nUse user1 and create a collection, let's name this collection1\nLogin as user2 and go to this link https://conifer.rhizome.org/api/v1/recordings?user=<user1>&coll=collection1\nSee that you can view all recordings of user1.\nImpact\nThis vulnerability is capable of viewing all users recordings.", "file_change_id": 161182946261793, "report_link": "https://huntr.com/bounties/4400a7d8-5934-4fe5-bf93-ccbb97edd818/", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "init_routes.reorder_bookmarks", "code_before": "        def reorder_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            new_order = request.json.get('order', [])\n\n            if blist.reorder_bookmarks(new_order):\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'success': True}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_order')\n", "code_after": "        def reorder_bookmarks(list_id):\n            user, collection, blist = self.load_user_coll_list(list_id)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            new_order = request.json.get('order', [])\n\n            if blist.reorder_bookmarks(new_order):\n                blist.mark_updated()\n                return {'success': True}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'invalid_order')\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described in the report is about an improper privilege escalation where any user can view all recordings of other users. The changes in the function \"init_routes\" are related to adding access control checks in the \"reorder_lists\" and \"reorder_bookmarks\" endpoints. \n\nIn the \"reorder_lists\" endpoint, a new line `self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)` has been added which seems to be checking if the current user has write access to the collection. Similarly, in the \"reorder_bookmarks\" endpoint, the same access control check has been added.\n\nThese changes seem to be directly addressing the reported vulnerability by adding necessary access control checks to prevent unauthorized access. Therefore, the function \"init_routes\" is vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "init_routes.list_pages", "code_before": "        def list_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            pages = collection.list_rec_pages(recording)\n            return {'pages': pages}\n", "code_after": "        def list_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            pages = collection.list_rec_pages(recording)\n            return {'pages': pages}\n", "commit_message": "Clarify and fix api access", "commit": "https://github.com/rhizome-conifer/conifer/commit/955948b0225c1d7498f83e7da8c592f69d077beb", "description": "Description\nHi there, I would like to report an improper privilege escalation in conifer. Any user can view all recordings of other users.\nProof of Concept\nGo to https://conifer.rhizome.org/ and register 2 accounts, let's call it user1 and user2\nUse user1 and create a collection, let's name this collection1\nLogin as user2 and go to this link https://conifer.rhizome.org/api/v1/recordings?user=<user1>&coll=collection1\nSee that you can view all recordings of user1.\nImpact\nThis vulnerability is capable of viewing all users recordings.", "file_change_id": 211553358343086, "report_link": "https://huntr.com/bounties/4400a7d8-5934-4fe5-bf93-ccbb97edd818/", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "init_routes.update_rec_desc", "code_before": "        def update_rec_desc(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            user.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording['desc'] = desc\n\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n", "code_after": "        def update_rec_desc(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording['desc'] = desc\n\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n"}, {"function_name": "init_routes", "code_before": "    def init_routes(self):\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Recordings')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recordings')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['title', 'desc'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def create_recording():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            title = data.get('title', '')\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording = collection.create_recording(title=title, desc=desc)\n\n            collection.mark_updated()\n\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recordings')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recordings')\n        def get_recordings():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            recs = collection.get_recordings()\n\n            return {'recordings': [rec.serialize() for rec in recs]}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def get_recording(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            if recording:\n                return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(404, 'no_such_recording')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['desc'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def update_rec_desc(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            user.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording['desc'] = desc\n\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_recording(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            errs = collection.remove_recording(recording, delete=True)\n            if errs.get('error'):\n                return self._raise_error(400, errs['error'])\n            else:\n                return {'deleted_id': rec}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/move/<new_coll_name>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='rec_move')\n        def move_recording(rec, new_coll_name):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            new_collection = user.get_collection_by_name(new_coll_name)\n            if not new_collection:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'no_such_collection')\n\n            user.access.assert_can_admin_coll(new_collection)\n\n            new_rec = collection.move_recording(recording, new_collection)\n\n            if new_rec:\n                collection.mark_updated()\n                new_collection.mark_updated()\n                return {'coll_id': new_coll_name, 'rec_id': new_rec}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'move_error')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/copy/<new_coll_name>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def copy_recording(rec, new_coll_name):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            new_collection = user.get_collection_by_name(new_coll_name)\n            if not new_collection:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'no_such_collection')\n\n            user.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n            user.access.assert_can_admin_coll(new_collection)\n\n            new_rec = new_collection.create_recording()\n\n            if new_rec.copy_data_from_recording(recording):\n                new_rec.mark_updated()\n                return {'recording': new_rec.serialize()}\n            else:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'copy_error')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['url', 'timestamp', 'title', 'browser'],\n                  resp='page_id')\n        def add_page(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            page_data = request.json or {}\n\n            page_id = collection.add_page(page_data, recording)\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'page_id': page_id}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='pages')\n        def list_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            pages = collection.list_rec_pages(recording)\n            return {'pages': pages}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/num_pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='count_pages')\n        def get_num_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            return {'count': recording.count_pages() }\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_page(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            url = request.json.get('url')\n            ts = request.json.get('timestamp')\n\n            return recording.delete_page(url, ts)\n\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag(None)\n", "code_after": "    def init_routes(self):\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Recordings')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recordings')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['title', 'desc'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def create_recording():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            title = data.get('title', '')\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording = collection.create_recording(title=title, desc=desc)\n\n            collection.mark_updated()\n\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recordings')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recordings')\n        def get_recordings():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            recs = collection.get_recordings()\n\n            return {'recordings': [rec.serialize() for rec in recs]}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def get_recording(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            if recording:\n                return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(404, 'no_such_recording')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['desc'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def update_rec_desc(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording['desc'] = desc\n\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_recording(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            errs = collection.remove_recording(recording, delete=True)\n            if errs.get('error'):\n                return self._raise_error(400, errs['error'])\n            else:\n                return {'deleted_id': rec}\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/move/<new_coll_name>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='rec_move')\n        def move_recording(rec, new_coll_name):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            new_collection = user.get_collection_by_name(new_coll_name)\n            if not new_collection:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'no_such_collection')\n\n            user.access.assert_can_admin_coll(new_collection)\n\n            new_rec = collection.move_recording(recording, new_collection)\n\n            if new_rec:\n                collection.mark_updated()\n                new_collection.mark_updated()\n                return {'coll_id': new_coll_name, 'rec_id': new_rec}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(400, 'move_error')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/copy/<new_coll_name>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def copy_recording(rec, new_coll_name):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            new_collection = user.get_collection_by_name(new_coll_name)\n            if not new_collection:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'no_such_collection')\n\n            user.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n            user.access.assert_can_admin_coll(new_collection)\n\n            new_rec = new_collection.create_recording()\n\n            if new_rec.copy_data_from_recording(recording):\n                new_rec.mark_updated()\n                return {'recording': new_rec.serialize()}\n            else:\n                return self._raise_error(400, 'copy_error')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['url', 'timestamp', 'title', 'browser'],\n                  resp='page_id')\n        def add_page(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            page_data = request.json or {}\n\n            page_id = collection.add_page(page_data, recording)\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'page_id': page_id}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='pages')\n        def list_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            pages = collection.list_rec_pages(recording)\n            return {'pages': pages}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/num_pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='count_pages')\n        def get_num_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            return {'count': recording.count_pages() }\n\n        @self.app.delete('/api/v1/recording/<rec>/pages')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='deleted')\n        def delete_page(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            url = request.json.get('url')\n            ts = request.json.get('timestamp')\n\n            return recording.delete_page(url, ts)\n\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag(None)\n"}, {"function_name": "init_routes.get_recordings", "code_before": "        def get_recordings():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            recs = collection.get_recordings()\n\n            return {'recordings': [rec.serialize() for rec in recs]}\n", "code_after": "        def get_recordings():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            recs = collection.get_recordings()\n\n            return {'recordings': [rec.serialize() for rec in recs]}\n"}, {"function_name": "init_routes.get_num_pages", "code_before": "        def get_num_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            return {'count': recording.count_pages() }\n", "code_after": "        def get_num_pages(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            self.access.assert_can_read_coll(collection)\n\n            return {'count': recording.count_pages() }\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The vulnerability described allows any user to view all recordings of other users. The change in the function \"init_routes.list_pages\" adds a line of code that asserts if the current user can read the collection. This change directly addresses the vulnerability by adding a permission check before listing the pages of a recording. \n\nThe other functions changed in the commit (\"init_routes.update_rec_desc\", \"init_routes\", \"init_routes.get_recordings\", \"init_routes.get_num_pages\") also add similar permission checks, indicating that the changes are part of a broader effort to fix the vulnerability across the system.\n\nTherefore, the function \"init_routes.list_pages\" is vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "init_routes", "code_before": "    def init_routes(self):\n        wr_api_spec.set_curr_tag('Recordings')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recordings')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['title', 'desc'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def create_recording():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            title = data.get('title', '')\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording = collection.create_recording(title=title, desc=desc)\n\n            collection.mark_updated()\n\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recordings')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recordings')\n        def get_recordings():\n            user, collection = self.load_user_coll()\n\n            recs = collection.get_recordings()\n\n            return {'recordings': [rec.serialize() for rec in recs]}\n\n        @self.app.get('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def get_recording(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            if recording:\n                return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n            else:\n                self._raise_error(404, 'no_such_recording')\n\n        @self.app.post('/api/v1/recording/<rec>')\n        @self.api(query=['user', 'coll'],\n                  req=['desc'],\n                  resp='recording')\n        def update_rec_desc(rec):\n            user, collection, recording = self.load_recording(rec)\n\n            user.access.assert_can_write_coll(collection)\n\n            data = request.json or {}\n\n            desc = data.get('desc', '')\n\n            recording['desc'] = desc\n\n            recording.mark_updated()\n            return {'recording': recording.serialize()}\n\n        @self.app.del