to show an error page, as the error might\n        # have happened when loading some resource.\n        # However, self.url() is not available yet and the requested URL\n        # might not match the URL we get from the error - so we just apply a\n        # heuristic here.\n        if (not qtutils.version_check('5.9') and\n                not error.ignore and\n                url.matches(self.url(requested=True), QUrl.RemoveScheme)):\n            self._show_error_page(url, str(error))\n", "commit_message": "Security: Remember hosts with ignored cert errors for load status\n\nWithout this change, we only set a flag when a certificate error occurred.\nHowever, when the same certificate error then happens a second time (e.g.\nbecause of a reload or opening the same URL again), we then colored the URL as\nsuccess_https (i.e. green) again.\n\nSee #5403\n\n(cherry picked from commit 021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975)", "commit": "https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/2281a205c3e70ec20f35ec8fafecee0d5c4f3478", "description": "# Description\n\nAfter a certificate error was overridden by the user, qutebrowser displays the URL as yellow (`colors.statusbar.url.warn.fg`). However, when the affected website was subsequently loaded again, the URL was mistakenly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`). While the user already has seen a certificate error prompt at this point (or set `content.ssl_strict` to `false` which is not recommended), this could still provide a false sense of security.\n\n# Affected versions and patches\n\nAll versions of qutebrowser are believed to be affected, though versions before v0.11.x couldn't be tested.\n\nThe issue is fixed in qutebrowser v1.11.1 (pending release) and v1.12.0 (unreleased). Backported patches for older versions are available, but no further releases are planned.\n\n# Mitigation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade:\n\n- Treat any host with a certificate exception as insecure, ignoring the URL color\n- Or set `content.ssl_strict` to `True` (instead of `'ask'`), preventing certificate exceptions\n\n# References\n\n- qutebrowser issue: https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/5403\n- Fix (master branch): https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975\n- Related issue for KDE Falkon: https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=420902\n- Related issue for eric6 Web Browser: https://tracker.die-offenbachs.homelinux.org/eric/issue328 (fixed in eric6 20.6)", "file_change_id": 125345733895939, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4rcq-jv2f-898j", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function \"_on_ssl_errors\" is indeed relevant to the vulnerability that the commit is trying to fix. The vulnerability is related to a false sense of security provided by the URL being displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`) after a certificate error was overridden by the user. \n\nIn the \"code_before\" section, the function sets a flag when a certificate error occurs. However, if the same certificate error happens again (for example, because of a reload or opening the same URL again), the URL is then colored as success_https (i.e., green) again.\n\nIn the \"code_after\" section, the function is changed to remember hosts with ignored certificate errors. This change is directly related to the vulnerability, as it addresses the issue of the URL being incorrectly displayed as green after a certificate error was overridden by the user.\n\nTherefore, the function \"_on_ssl_errors\" is vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_on_ssl_errors", "code_before": "    def _on_ssl_errors(self):\n        self._has_ssl_errors = True\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "Security: Remember hosts with ignored cert errors for load status\n\nWithout this change, we only set a flag when a certificate error occurred.\nHowever, when the same certificate error then happens a second time (e.g.\nbecause of a reload or opening the same URL again), we then colored the URL as\nsuccess_https (i.e. green) again.\n\nSee #5403\n\n(cherry picked from commit 021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975)", "commit": "https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/2281a205c3e70ec20f35ec8fafecee0d5c4f3478", "description": "# Description\n\nAfter a certificate error was overridden by the user, qutebrowser displays the URL as yellow (`colors.statusbar.url.warn.fg`). However, when the affected website was subsequently loaded again, the URL was mistakenly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`). While the user already has seen a certificate error prompt at this point (or set `content.ssl_strict` to `false` which is not recommended), this could still provide a false sense of security.\n\n# Affected versions and patches\n\nAll versions of qutebrowser are believed to be affected, though versions before v0.11.x couldn't be tested.\n\nThe issue is fixed in qutebrowser v1.11.1 (pending release) and v1.12.0 (unreleased). Backported patches for older versions are available, but no further releases are planned.\n\n# Mitigation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade:\n\n- Treat any host with a certificate exception as insecure, ignoring the URL color\n- Or set `content.ssl_strict` to `True` (instead of `'ask'`), preventing certificate exceptions\n\n# References\n\n- qutebrowser issue: https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/5403\n- Fix (master branch): https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975\n- Related issue for KDE Falkon: https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=420902\n- Related issue for eric6 Web Browser: https://tracker.die-offenbachs.homelinux.org/eric/issue328 (fixed in eric6 20.6)", "file_change_id": 121744821990543, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4rcq-jv2f-898j", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `_on_ssl_errors` was removed in the commit. The commit message suggests that the change was made to address a security issue where the URL was incorrectly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`) after a certificate error was overridden by the user, providing a false sense of security.\n\nThe function `_on_ssl_errors` was setting a flag `_has_ssl_errors` to `True` when SSL errors occurred. The removal of this function suggests that it was part of the mechanism that was incorrectly handling the certificate errors, leading to the security issue.\n\nGiven this, it can be inferred that the function `_on_ssl_errors` was vulnerable and was changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_on_load_finished", "code_before": "    def _on_load_finished(self, ok):\n        if sip.isdeleted(self._widget):\n            # https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/3498\n            return\n\n        sess_manager = objreg.get('session-manager')\n        sess_manager.save_autosave()\n\n        if ok and not self._has_ssl_errors:\n            if self.url().scheme() == 'https':\n                self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.success_https)\n            else:\n                self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.success)\n        elif ok:\n            self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.warn)\n        else:\n            self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.error)\n\n        self.load_finished.emit(ok)\n\n        if not self.title():\n            self.title_changed.emit(self.url().toDisplayString())\n\n        self.zoom.set_current()\n", "code_after": "    def _on_load_finished(self, ok):\n        if sip.isdeleted(self._widget):\n            # https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/3498\n            return\n\n        sess_manager = objreg.get('session-manager')\n        sess_manager.save_autosave()\n\n        if ok:\n            if self.url().scheme() == 'https':\n                if self.url().host() in self._insecure_hosts:\n                    self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.warn)\n                else:\n                    self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.success_https)\n            else:\n                self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.success)\n        elif ok:\n            self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.warn)\n        else:\n            self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.error)\n\n        self.load_finished.emit(ok)\n\n        if not self.title():\n            self.title_changed.emit(self.url().toDisplayString())\n\n        self.zoom.set_current()\n", "commit_message": "Security: Remember hosts with ignored cert errors for load status\n\nWithout this change, we only set a flag when a certificate error occurred.\nHowever, when the same certificate error then happens a second time (e.g.\nbecause of a reload or opening the same URL again), we then colored the URL as\nsuccess_https (i.e. green) again.\n\nSee #5403\n\n(cherry picked from commit 021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975)", "commit": "https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/4020210b193f77cf1785b21717f6ef7c5de5f0f8", "description": "# Description\n\nAfter a certificate error was overridden by the user, qutebrowser displays the URL as yellow (`colors.statusbar.url.warn.fg`). However, when the affected website was subsequently loaded again, the URL was mistakenly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`). While the user already has seen a certificate error prompt at this point (or set `content.ssl_strict` to `false` which is not recommended), this could still provide a false sense of security.\n\n# Affected versions and patches\n\nAll versions of qutebrowser are believed to be affected, though versions before v0.11.x couldn't be tested.\n\nThe issue is fixed in qutebrowser v1.11.1 (pending release) and v1.12.0 (unreleased). Backported patches for older versions are available, but no further releases are planned.\n\n# Mitigation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade:\n\n- Treat any host with a certificate exception as insecure, ignoring the URL color\n- Or set `content.ssl_strict` to `True` (instead of `'ask'`), preventing certificate exceptions\n\n# References\n\n- qutebrowser issue: https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/5403\n- Fix (master branch): https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975\n- Related issue for KDE Falkon: https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=420902\n- Related issue for eric6 Web Browser: https://tracker.die-offenbachs.homelinux.org/eric/issue328 (fixed in eric6 20.6)", "file_change_id": 2365457365347, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4rcq-jv2f-898j", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [{"function_name": "__init__", "code_before": "    def __init__(self, *, win_id, mode_manager, private, parent=None):\n        self.private = private\n        self.win_id = win_id\n        self.tab_id = next(tab_id_gen)\n        super().__init__(parent)\n\n        self.registry = objreg.ObjectRegistry()\n        tab_registry = objreg.get('tab-registry', scope='window',\n                                  window=win_id)\n        tab_registry[self.tab_id] = self\n        objreg.register('tab', self, registry=self.registry)\n\n        self.data = TabData()\n        self._layout = miscwidgets.WrapperLayout(self)\n        self._widget = None\n        self._progress = 0\n        self._has_ssl_errors = False\n        self._mode_manager = mode_manager\n        self._load_status = usertypes.LoadStatus.none\n        self._mouse_event_filter = mouse.MouseEventFilter(\n            self, parent=self)\n        self.backend = None\n\n        # FIXME:qtwebengine  Should this be public api via self.hints?\n        #                    Also, should we get it out of objreg?\n        hintmanager = hints.HintManager(win_id, self.tab_id, parent=self)\n        objreg.register('hintmanager', hintmanager, scope='tab',\n                        window=self.win_id, tab=self.tab_id)\n\n        self.predicted_navigation.connect(self._on_predicted_navigation)\n", "code_after": "    def __init__(self, *, win_id, mode_manager, private, parent=None):\n        self.private = private\n        self.win_id = win_id\n        self.tab_id = next(tab_id_gen)\n        super().__init__(parent)\n\n        self.registry = objreg.ObjectRegistry()\n        tab_registry = objreg.get('tab-registry', scope='window',\n                                  window=win_id)\n        tab_registry[self.tab_id] = self\n        objreg.register('tab', self, registry=self.registry)\n\n        self.data = TabData()\n        self._layout = miscwidgets.WrapperLayout(self)\n        self._widget = None\n        self._progress = 0\n        self._mode_manager = mode_manager\n        self._load_status = usertypes.LoadStatus.none\n        self._mouse_event_filter = mouse.MouseEventFilter(\n            self, parent=self)\n        self.backend = None\n\n        # FIXME:qtwebengine  Should this be public api via self.hints?\n        #                    Also, should we get it out of objreg?\n        hintmanager = hints.HintManager(win_id, self.tab_id, parent=self)\n        objreg.register('hintmanager', hintmanager, scope='tab',\n                        window=self.win_id, tab=self.tab_id)\n\n        self.predicted_navigation.connect(self._on_predicted_navigation)\n"}, {"function_name": "_on_load_started", "code_before": "    def _on_load_started(self):\n        self._progress = 0\n        self._has_ssl_errors = False\n        self.data.viewing_source = False\n        self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.loading)\n        self.load_started.emit()\n", "code_after": "    def _on_load_started(self):\n        self._progress = 0\n        self.data.viewing_source = False\n        self._set_load_status(usertypes.LoadStatus.loading)\n        self.load_started.emit()\n"}], "gpt_answer": "The function \"_on_load_finished\" is indeed relevant to the vulnerability that the commit is trying to fix. The vulnerability is about the URL being mistakenly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`) after a certificate error was overridden by the user. \n\nIn the \"code_after\" section, we can see that a new condition has been added to check if the host of the URL is in the list of insecure hosts. If it is, the load status is set to warning, which would presumably change the color of the URL to yellow, indicating a potential security risk. This change directly addresses the vulnerability described.\n\nTherefore, the function \"_on_load_finished\" was vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_on_ssl_errors", "code_before": "    def _on_ssl_errors(self, error):\n        self._has_ssl_errors = True\n\n        url = error.url()\n        log.webview.debug(\"Certificate error: {}\".format(error))\n\n        if error.is_overridable():\n            error.ignore = shared.ignore_certificate_errors(\n                url, [error], abort_on=[self.shutting_down, self.load_started])\n        else:\n            log.webview.error(\"Non-overridable certificate error: \"\n                              \"{}\".format(error))\n\n        log.webview.debug(\"ignore {}, URL {}, requested {}\".format(\n            error.ignore, url, self.url(requested=True)))\n\n        # WORKAROUND for https://bugreports.qt.io/browse/QTBUG-56207\n        # We can't really know when to show an error page, as the error might\n        # have happened when loading some resource.\n        # However, self.url() is not available yet and the requested URL\n        # might not match the URL we get from the error - so we just apply a\n        # heuristic here.\n        if (not qtutils.version_check('5.9') and\n                not error.ignore and\n                url.matches(self.url(requested=True), QUrl.RemoveScheme)):\n            self._show_error_page(url, str(error))\n", "code_after": "    def _on_ssl_errors(self, error):\n        url = error.url()\n        self._insecure_hosts.add(url.host())\n\n        log.webview.debug(\"Certificate error: {}\".format(error))\n\n        if error.is_overridable():\n            error.ignore = shared.ignore_certificate_errors(\n                url, [error], abort_on=[self.shutting_down, self.load_started])\n        else:\n            log.webview.error(\"Non-overridable certificate error: \"\n                              \"{}\".format(error))\n\n        log.webview.debug(\"ignore {}, URL {}, requested {}\".format(\n            error.ignore, url, self.url(requested=True)))\n\n        # WORKAROUND for https://bugreports.qt.io/browse/QTBUG-56207\n        # We can't really know when to show an error page, as the error might\n        # have happened when loading some resource.\n        # However, self.url() is not available yet and the requested URL\n        # might not match the URL we get from the error - so we just apply a\n        # heuristic here.\n        if (not qtutils.version_check('5.9') and\n                not error.ignore and\n                url.matches(self.url(requested=True), QUrl.RemoveScheme)):\n            self._show_error_page(url, str(error))\n", "commit_message": "Security: Remember hosts with ignored cert errors for load status\n\nWithout this change, we only set a flag when a certificate error occurred.\nHowever, when the same certificate error then happens a second time (e.g.\nbecause of a reload or opening the same URL again), we then colored the URL as\nsuccess_https (i.e. green) again.\n\nSee #5403\n\n(cherry picked from commit 021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975)", "commit": "https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/4020210b193f77cf1785b21717f6ef7c5de5f0f8", "description": "# Description\n\nAfter a certificate error was overridden by the user, qutebrowser displays the URL as yellow (`colors.statusbar.url.warn.fg`). However, when the affected website was subsequently loaded again, the URL was mistakenly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`). While the user already has seen a certificate error prompt at this point (or set `content.ssl_strict` to `false` which is not recommended), this could still provide a false sense of security.\n\n# Affected versions and patches\n\nAll versions of qutebrowser are believed to be affected, though versions before v0.11.x couldn't be tested.\n\nThe issue is fixed in qutebrowser v1.11.1 (pending release) and v1.12.0 (unreleased). Backported patches for older versions are available, but no further releases are planned.\n\n# Mitigation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade:\n\n- Treat any host with a certificate exception as insecure, ignoring the URL color\n- Or set `content.ssl_strict` to `True` (instead of `'ask'`), preventing certificate exceptions\n\n# References\n\n- qutebrowser issue: https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/5403\n- Fix (master branch): https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975\n- Related issue for KDE Falkon: https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=420902\n- Related issue for eric6 Web Browser: https://tracker.die-offenbachs.homelinux.org/eric/issue328 (fixed in eric6 20.6)", "file_change_id": 13256580661985, "report_link": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4rcq-jv2f-898j", "programming_language": "Python", "other_changed_function_in_the_commit": [], "gpt_answer": "The provided information indicates that the function `_on_ssl_errors` is indeed relevant to the vulnerability that the commit is trying to fix. The vulnerability is related to the incorrect display of URL color after a certificate error was overridden by the user, which could provide a false sense of security.\n\nIn the `code_before` and `code_after` snippets, we can see that the function `_on_ssl_errors` has been modified. The change involves removing the line `self._has_ssl_errors = True` and adding `self._insecure_hosts.add(url.host())`. This change seems to be directly related to the vulnerability, as it now keeps track of hosts with ignored certificate errors, which is crucial for correctly displaying the URL color.\n\nTherefore, the function `_on_ssl_errors` appears to be vulnerable and has been changed to fix the vulnerability.\n\n```json\n{\n\"answer\": 1\n}\n```"}
{"function_name": "_on_ssl_errors", "code_before": "    def _on_ssl_errors(self):\n        self._has_ssl_errors = True\n", "code_after": "", "commit_message": "Security: Remember hosts with ignored cert errors for load status\n\nWithout this change, we only set a flag when a certificate error occurred.\nHowever, when the same certificate error then happens a second time (e.g.\nbecause of a reload or opening the same URL again), we then colored the URL as\nsuccess_https (i.e. green) again.\n\nSee #5403\n\n(cherry picked from commit 021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975)", "commit": "https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/4020210b193f77cf1785b21717f6ef7c5de5f0f8", "description": "# Description\n\nAfter a certificate error was overridden by the user, qutebrowser displays the URL as yellow (`colors.statusbar.url.warn.fg`). However, when the affected website was subsequently loaded again, the URL was mistakenly displayed as green (`colors.statusbar.url.success_https`). While the user already has seen a certificate error prompt at this point (or set `content.ssl_strict` to `false` which is not recommended), this could still provide a false sense of security.\n\n# Affected versions and patches\n\nAll versions of qutebrowser are believed to be affected, though versions before v0.11.x couldn't be tested.\n\nThe issue is fixed in qutebrowser v1.11.1 (pending release) and v1.12.0 (unreleased). Backported patches for older versions are available, but no further releases are planned.\n\n# Mitigation\n\nIf you are unable to upgrade:\n\n- Treat any host with a certificate exception as insecure, ignoring the URL color\n- Or set `content.ssl_strict` to `True` (instead of `'ask'`), preventing certificate exceptions\n\n# References\n\n- qutebrowser issue: https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/issues/5403\n- Fix (master branch): https://github.com/qutebrowser/qutebrowser/commit/021ab572a319ca3db5907a33a59774f502b3b975\n- Related issue for KDE Falkon: https://bugs.kde